#!/usr/bin/python3
"""kawa-panel — the panels under the bar: quick settings (its cat, or Super+X), the sound panel
(the bar's volume, or Super+A) and the brightness slider (the bar's brightness).

    kawa-panel               run, hidden, for the whole session (autostart starts it): it opens
                             at once when asked, through its fifo (below)
    kawa-panel --open        quick settings: open, or closed if open
    kawa-panel --sound       the sound panel: open, or closed if it is open
    kawa-panel --brightness  the brightness slider: open, or closed if it is open
(each asks the running panel through its fifo, or, with none running, becomes it, open; the
bar and the keys ask through kawa-quick, which needs no Python to start.) One panel or menu at
a time: opening one closes the other.

Asked through a fifo, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kawa-panel.in, one line a request (each written whole,
in one write: none is lost or merged, however fast they come), answered in the order they came:
    toggle MODE       quick, sound or brightness: open, or closed if it is the one open
    menu PID          kawa-menu's (PID: its pid): the menu in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kawa-menu.PID.req
                      (its caller, the number of options, the options, the rows — a line each),
                      answered into the fifo kawa-menu.PID.out: "RC TEXT" (0 and the chosen
                      line, or 1 dismissed, 2 wrong use); kawa-menu ending first takes it away
    close             whatever is open closes (the launcher is opening)
A request is a toggle: the panel or menu (a menu by the script that asked for it) open closes
instead. Whether it came from a click on the bar or from a key is known exactly: dwl rewrites
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dwl-pointer at every press on the bar, before the bar hears of it, so a
request that finds it fresh and not yet answered is that click's. A click outside a menu closes
it (dwl takes the keyboard back); when that click was on the bar, the click is remembered, and
the request it then makes for that same menu is that close (it stays closed) — a later click
is another click, however fast it comes. Every key's request counts, however fast they come: dwl
repeats no held key that opens something (one press), so presses as fast as you like end open
after an odd number, closed after an even one.

Quick settings, top to bottom, on one 16 px grid (the day, the time, the weather and the
volume's number are the bar's, right above it, so they are not said again here):
    two meters    明 brightness and 音 sound: a slider each (their levels are the
                  bar's), or the words when there is no level; Enter (or a click on its
                  name) on 音 sound opens the sound panel
    the tiles     theme · night light · freesync · network · bluetooth · devices (those two
                  only with a Bluetooth adapter) · drives · backup · dnd · screensaver — each
                  its icon, a kanji seal, its name and how it stands; the seal is stamped
                  vermilion while the thing is on (the night light: its warmth, the wheel over
                  it warmer or cooler; the screensaver: on or off)
    three keys    wallpaper · screenshot · power (its icon alone), centred
The sound panel, three parts under kanji seals: 音 sound — the volume's slider (its fill hollow
while muted; its icon the bar's) and two keys, mute and mixer (wiremix) · 出 output and
入 input — every device (not "Share"), the one in use ticked: a click makes another the
default, playing audio moves to it · the input's foot: while the clean mic is on, its level
live (a meter that only shows: pw-record reads it while the panel is open, nothing after),
mute microphone, then clean mic ▣/□ (the default input clean ⇄ raw, at once; what records
moves with it) and test mic (5 s recorded, played back: kawa-quick --do mictest).
The brightness slider: the brightness meter alone, as in quick settings.
Opened by a click on the bar, each hangs right under the pointer (dwl's dwl-pointer, written
as it clicks), kept 8 px inside its output; opened by its key, under its own module: quick
settings at the right end, the others under the volume and the brightness (fixed margins,
measured on a 1920 px bar).

The pointer draws no box: the item under it keeps its look, only its icon (a meter's kanji)
and its name turn vermilion — at once, no easing, also right after it opens. A click does
what is under the pointer. The keyboard's cursor (filled in vermilion) is not there until a
key asks for it: the first arrow, Tab or Enter puts it on the first item, every open starts
without it (nothing remembered from the last time).

Keys: arrows or h j k l move · on a meter ← → (h l) change it by 5 (the slider's from the
first press: it is all there is) · Enter or Space does it · Tab goes round · Esc closes.
The mouse: a click does it; on a meter a click or a drag sets the level there, the wheel moves
it by 5. A click outside closes it, as with every menu. Switches (theme, night light,
freesync, bluetooth, dnd, screensaver; mute, an output, an input) stay open and show the new state; the
rest close it and open their own menu or window. Everything is done by kawa-quick (--state,
--do), which knows the machine; this only draws it and listens.

While open each says so to the bar: a marker holding its pid, kawa-panel.open (the cat),
kawa-panel-sound.open and kawa-panel-brightness.open, and a poke (SIGRTMIN+15, +16, +14).
Its colours are the theme's (~/.config/fedokawaii/current/panel.conf): a theme switch
restyles it at once. Exit: 0 · 2 wrong use.
"""
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import sys
import time
import unicodedata

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
CURRENT = os.path.join(HOME, ".config/fedokawaii/current")
GLASS = os.path.join(HOME, ".local/state/fedokawaii/foot-glass.ini")   # the terminal's see-through ground
ICONS_SH = os.path.join(HOME, ".config/fedokawaii/icons.sh")
RUN = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR") or f"/run/user/{os.getuid()}"
PIDFILE = os.path.join(RUN, "kawa-panel.pid")
FIFO = os.path.join(RUN, "kawa-panel.in")           # the requests (above)
MODES = ("quick", "sound", "brightness")
# the markers: each holds the panel's pid while that mode is open (one left by a panel gone is
# no open panel). kawa-panel.open lights the bar's cat (kawa-bar) — its own: a
# dropdown's (kawa-drop.open) is kawa-menu's
MARKS = {"quick": os.path.join(RUN, "kawa-panel.open"),
         "sound": os.path.join(RUN, "kawa-panel-sound.open"),
         "brightness": os.path.join(RUN, "kawa-panel-brightness.open"),
         "launcher": os.path.join(RUN, "kawa-launcher.open")}   # (the torii's)
MARK = MARKS["quick"]
POKE = {"quick": 15, "sound": 16, "brightness": 14, "launcher": 20}  # the bar's module told (SIGRTMIN+N)
# where each hangs, 8 px under the bar. Opened by a click on the bar: centred under the
# pointer, on its output, kept EDGE px inside the output's edges (dwl writes where the click
# was, POINTER: "X Y OUTPUT OUTPUT_X OUTPUT_WIDTH", before waybar runs the module's command —
# fresh: under FRESH s old). Opened by its key: its right edge EDGE px from the screen's (the
# gap the windows keep) — every one: the bar's modules stand within a panel's width of its
# right end (the speaker's ink at x 1674 on a 1920 px bar, a 412 px panel 24 px from it would
# end past the screen's edge)
POINTER = os.path.join(RUN, "dwl-pointer")
FRESH = 1.0
EDGE = 8
TITLE = {"quick": "quick settings", "sound": "sound", "brightness": "brightness", "menu": "menu"}
COUNTS = os.path.join(HOME, ".cache/fedokawaii/launcher-counts")   # how often each entry was started
# a dropdown's button (kawa-bar): kawa-drop.open holds this panel's pid and the script that
# asked for the menu while it is open; its module is poked (SIGRTMIN+N), the timer's woken
DROP = os.path.join(RUN, "kawa-drop.open")
DROP_POKE = {"kawa-calendar": "19", "kawa-drives": "13", "kawa-steam": "17", "kawa-weather": "18",
             "kawa-tag": "9", "kawa-timer": None, "kawa-downloads": "21"}

# ── the grid (pixels) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# (docs/design-system.md §3; Sumi UI at 14 px — a 7 px column a letter; the rest of this grid is
# the old one until T13 lays the panels on the 8 × 20 cell)
BORDER, PAD = 2, 16                  # the frame and the margin inside it
TILE_W, TILE_H, GAP = 120, 80, 8     # three tiles a row: 3 × 120 + 2 × 8 = 376, the content
INNER = 3 * TILE_W + 2 * GAP
COL = 7                              # a column of Sumi UI: a letter (a kanji or kana: two)
LINE = 14                            # a line of text, Sumi UI (its em)
SEAL = 26                            # a seal: a 16 px kanji, 4 px of paper and a 1 px frame all round
RULE = 29                            # a section's rule: 14 px, the stitch, 14 px
METER_H, METER_GAP = 24, 2           # a row (its words 4 px from the top), 2 px between rows
SPAN, KNOB, KNOB_H, FILL_H, TRACK_H = 190, 4, 14, 6, 2   # a meter's slider (below: one rule both ways)
KEY_H = 32                           # a key button (quick settings' foot: 164 · 164 · 32, power)
SECTION = 24                         # the sound panel: a part's last row to the next seal
NAME_X = GAP + LINE + GAP            # a row's name: its glyph's column, then a gap
STAMP = "outline"                    # how a tile's seal is stamped (see seal() in main)



def icons(path=ICONS_SH):
    """Each icon by its name (moon, sun…) → its character, from icons.sh (tools/build-icons.py
    writes both)."""
    out = {}
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("ICON_") and "=$'" in line:
            k, v = line.split("=", 1)
            raw = v[2:v.index("'", 2)]
            out[k[5:].lower()] = bytes(int(h, 16) for h in raw.split("\\x")[1:]).decode("utf-8")
    return out


def glass():
    """The terminal's ground's alpha (kawa-glass: Super+U → transparency), which the panels
    take too — on as it is made (0.92), a file without an alpha line: opaque."""
    try:
        with open(GLASS, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith("alpha="):
                    return max(0.0, min(1.0, float(line[6:])))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return 0.92
    return 1.0


def colours(path):
    """panel.conf: NAME=rrggbb → NAME: (r, g, b) in 0…1."""
    out = {}
    for line in open(path, encoding="utf-8"):
        line = line.strip()
        if line and not line.startswith("#") and "=" in line:
            k, v = line.split("=", 1)
            out[k] = tuple(int(v[i:i + 2], 16) / 255 for i in (0, 2, 4))
    return out


def watch_theme(Gio, changed):
    """changed() each time ~/.config/fedokawaii/current is re-pointed (kawa-theme renames a new
    link over it): its folder watched, moves seen as one event. Returns the monitor (kept)."""
    mon = Gio.File.new_for_path(os.path.dirname(CURRENT)).monitor_directory(Gio.FileMonitorFlags.WATCH_MOVES, None)

    def on_dir(m, f, other, ev):
        if os.path.basename((other or f).get_path()) == "current" or f.get_basename() == "current":
            changed()
    mon.connect("changed", on_dir)
    return mon


def parse_state(text):
    s = {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if "\t" in line:
            k, v = line.split("\t", 1)
            s[k] = v
    return s


# ── a sound device's short name ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PipeWire names a device after its card's chip ("Ryzen HD Audio Controller Analog Stereo",
# "Navi 48 HDMI/DP Audio Controller Digital Stereo (HDMI)"): long, alike, and saying nothing
# of where the sound goes. The panel says it shortly, from PipeWire's own words, in this
# order: the port in use ("Line Out" → line out, "Rear Microphone" → rear mic) — but an
# HDMI port says only HDMI —, the nick when it is a name (an HDMI output's: the monitor's,
# "LG ULTRAGEAR"; a chip's, "ALC1220 Analog", is not), the card when it is a product (a USB
# microphone's, "Yeti Stereo Microphone"; a controller is not). An output adds its form
# (analog, HDMI, S/PDIF, bluetooth): "line out · analog", "LG ULTRAGEAR · HDMI"; an input
# needs none ("rear mic"). Lower case but for names and abbreviations. Two alike are told
# apart by their cards ("HDMI · Navi 48", "HDMI · Radeon"), else by number.
CHIP_CARD = re.compile(r"\b(controller|hd audio|high definition audio|audio device|hdmi/dp|generic)\b", re.I)
CHIP_NICK = re.compile(r"\b(hdmi|hda|dp|analog|digital|alc\d+\w*|usb audio|s/pdif|iec958)\b", re.I)
BARE_PORT = re.compile(r"^(analog|digital) (output|input)$|hdmi|displayport|s/pdif|iec958", re.I)
CARD_WORDS = re.compile(r"\b(hd|high|definition|audio|controller|hdmi/dp|device|generic)\b", re.I)


def lower_words(t):
    """Lower case, but for abbreviations (USB, S/PDIF) and numbers."""
    return " ".join(w if (w.isupper() and len(w) > 1) or "/" in w else w.lower() for w in t.split())


def form_of(profile, desc, card, api):
    """An output's form, from its profile's description (or its description's tail)."""
    if api == "bluez5":
        return "bluetooth"
    p = profile or (desc[len(card):].strip() if card and desc.startswith(card) else "")
    if not p:
        m = re.search(r"\b((?:Analog|Digital|Pro)\b.*)$", desc)
        p = m.group(1) if m else ""
    m = re.fullmatch(r"Digital [\w. ]*\((.+)\)", p)
    if m:
        return "S/PDIF" if m.group(1) == "IEC958" else m.group(1)
    m = re.fullmatch(r"Analog (Stereo|Mono|Surround ([\d.]+))", p)
    if m:
        return "analog" + (f" {m.group(2)}" if m.group(2) else "")
    return lower_words(p) if p else ""


def port_of(port):
    """The port in use, said shortly ("Rear Microphone" → rear mic); none for a port that says
    no more than the form (HDMI / DisplayPort, Digital Output (S/PDIF), Analog Output)."""
    if not port or BARE_PORT.search(port):
        return ""
    return lower_words(re.sub(r"\bMicrophone\b", "mic", port))


def device_name(kind, desc, port="", nick="", card="", profile="", api=""):
    """A device's short name (see above): kind is output or input."""
    first = (port_of(port)
             or (nick if nick and nick != desc and not CHIP_NICK.search(nick) else "")
             or (card if card and not CHIP_CARD.search(card) else "")
             or (desc if api == "bluez5" else ""))
    form = form_of(profile, desc, card, api)
    if kind == "input":
        return first or form or desc
    return " · ".join(x for x in (first, form) if x) or desc


def card_short(card):
    """A card's own words, without the chip's ("Navi 48 HDMI/DP Audio Controller" → Navi 48)."""
    return " ".join(CARD_WORDS.sub(" ", card).split())


def device_names(devs):
    """Each device's short name — those alike told apart: by their cards if those differ, else
    by number (2, 3… after the first). devs: [(kind, desc, port, nick, card, profile, api)]."""
    names = [device_name(*d) for d in devs]
    for n in set(names):
        same = [i for i, m in enumerate(names) if m == n]
        if len(same) < 2:
            continue
        cards = [card_short(devs[i][4]) for i in same]
        by_card = all(cards) and len(set(cards)) == len(cards)
        for k, i in enumerate(same):
            names[i] = f"{n} · {cards[k]}" if by_card else (n if k == 0 else f"{n} {k + 1}")
    return names


def parse_sound(text):
    """kawa-quick --state sound: sound (its level, also while muted), muted (on|off), mic
    (on|muted|none) as KEY<TAB>VALUE; each device a line "output|input<TAB>ID<TAB>1|0 (the
    default)<TAB>DESCRIPTION<TAB>PORT<TAB>NICK<TAB>CARD<TAB>PROFILE<TAB>API" → outputs and
    inputs, in order, each (id, default, its short name: device_names)."""
    s = {"outputs": [], "inputs": []}
    devs = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        f = line.split("\t")
        if f[0] in ("output", "input") and len(f) == 9 and f[1].isdigit():
            devs.append(f)
        elif len(f) == 2:
            s[f[0]] = f[1]
    for kind in ("output", "input"):                 # (alike within a list: the outputs, the inputs)
        mine = [f for f in devs if f[0] == kind]
        for f, name in zip(mine, device_names([(f[0], *f[3:]) for f in mine])):
            s[kind + "s"].append((f[1], f[2] == "1", name))
    return s


def percent(v):
    """'70%' → 70; anything else (no DDC/CI, muted, no output) → None."""
    return int(v[:-1]) if v.endswith("%") and v[:-1].isdigit() else None


def peak_pct(data):
    """A block of 16-bit samples (the clean mic's, pw-record's) → the meter's level: its peak
    on a 60 dB scale (−60 dBFS and below 0, full scale 100: the rule full is clipping)."""
    import array
    import math
    a = array.array("h", data[:len(data) // 2 * 2])
    pk = max((abs(v) for v in a), default=0)
    if pk == 0:
        return 0
    return max(0, min(100, round((20 * math.log10(pk / 32768) + 60) * 100 / 60)))


def vol_icon(pct, muted):
    """The sound panel's meter's icon: the bar's own for that level (its speaker with no wave
    at 0, one up to 25 %, two to 50, three to 75, four above; crossed out while muted)."""
    if muted:
        return "vol_mute"
    if not pct:
        return "vol_0"
    return f"vol_{min(4, (pct + 24) // 25)}"


# ── a meter's slider and its level: one rule both ways ────────────────────────────────────
# A 190 px rule: the track (2 px, faint) filled 6 px tall in asagi up to the level, the knob —
# an ink rod, 4 × 14 — centred on the fill's end with a pixel of air either side, a tick (1 × 6)
# at each quarter of the track still showing. The level and the pixels are each other's
# inverse: a level fills fill_of(level) pixels (to the nearest, 1 % ≈ 1.9 px); a pointer `off`
# pixels into the rule means the level whose fill ends there — the knob's middle is always
# under the pointer. Left of the rule is 0, its end and beyond 100. kawa-osd draws the same
# pixels from its glyphs (art/icons.txt: lv-*).


def fill_of(pct):
    """How many pixels of the rule a level fills (0…190); above 100 % (the mixer's) all."""
    if pct is None or pct <= 0:
        return 0
    return min(SPAN, (pct * SPAN * 2 + 100) // 200)


def knob_of(pct):
    """Where the knob starts on the rule: centred on the fill's end, kept on the rule."""
    return max(0, min(SPAN - KNOB, fill_of(pct) - KNOB // 2))


def ticks():
    """The quarter ticks' columns on the rule."""
    return [(SPAN * q * 2 + 4) // 8 for q in (1, 2, 3)]


def level_at(off):
    """The level a pointer `off` pixels right of the rule's left edge means (any number: a
    drag past either end holds that end)."""
    o = int(off // 1)
    if o <= 0:
        return 0
    if o >= SPAN:
        return 100
    return min(100, (o * 200 + SPAN) // (2 * SPAN))


def snap(now, d):
    """A key's or the wheel's step from `now`: to the next multiple of |d| that way (62 +5 → 65,
    62 −5 → 60), within 0…100."""
    a = abs(d)
    v = (now // a + 1) * a if d > 0 else ((now + a - 1) // a - 1) * a
    return max(0, min(100, v))


def bar_click(text, age):
    """dwl's record of the last click on the bar (POINTER's text, age seconds old) → (x, the
    output's x, its width), or None: too old (not this opening's: a key opened it), or not
    such a record."""
    if not -FRESH < age < FRESH:
        return None
    f = text.split()
    if len(f) != 5:
        return None
    try:
        return int(float(f[0])), int(f[3]), int(f[4])
    except ValueError:
        return None


def hang(x, out_x, out_w, w):
    """The left margin, on its output, of a panel w wide centred under x (the layout's x):
    kept EDGE px inside the output's edges (the bar's last icons: the panel hangs from the
    edge instead)."""
    return max(EDGE, min(out_w - w - EDGE, x - out_x - w // 2))


def where(click, w):
    """Where the panel hangs: ("left", margin, the output's x) under a click, else ("right",
    EDGE, None)."""
    if click:
        x, out_x, out_x_w = click
        return "left", hang(x, out_x, out_x_w, w), out_x
    return "right", EDGE, None


def menu_place(drop, click, w):
    """A menu's: dropped from the bar (--drop) as a panel is — under the click, or 8 px from
    the right edge —, any other in the middle of the screen."""
    return where(click, w) if drop else ("centre", 0, None)


def real_leave(mode, detail):
    """Whether a leave-notify (GDK's crossing mode and detail, by their nicks) is the pointer
    really gone: not into a child's window (inferior), not a grab taking the pointer while it
    stays over the panel (grab, gtk-grab, state-changed) — those leave it where it is."""
    return detail != "inferior" and mode not in ("grab", "gtk-grab", "state-changed")


# ── what is shown ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
class Item:
    def __init__(self, key, kind, x, y, w, h, row, **kw):
        self.key, self.kind, self.x, self.y, self.w, self.h, self.row = key, kind, x, y, w, h, row
        self.kanji = self.icon = self.glyph = None
        self.lit = self.muted = self.scrolls = False
        self.name = self.value = self.tip = self.detail = ""
        self.__dict__.update(kw)

    def inside(self, x, y):
        return self.x <= x < self.x + self.w and self.y <= y < self.y + self.h

    def centre(self):
        return self.x + self.w / 2


def vrr_shown(s):
    """FreeSync's tile: the setting and what the card does this moment (vrrnow, DRM's
    VRR_ENABLED) — "games · off" at the desktop, "games · on" in a full-screen game, "always ·
    on"; stamped while it is on. "not offered": no screen offers it (the monitor's own menu)."""
    want, now = s.get("vrr", "in games"), s.get("vrrnow")
    if want == "off":
        return "off", False
    if now == "none":
        return "not offered", False
    if now in ("on", "off"):
        return ("games" if want == "in games" else want) + " · " + now, now == "on"
    return want, True                      # (unread: the setting alone, as it was asked)


def tiles_of(s):
    """(key, icon, kanji, name, value, stamped) for each tile, in order."""
    dark = s.get("theme", "dark") != "light"
    net = s.get("nettype", "offline")
    t = [("theme", "moon" if dark else "sun", "夜" if dark else "昼", "theme", "dark" if dark else "light", True),
         ("night", "lantern", "灯", "night light", s.get("night", "off"), s.get("night", "off") != "off"),
         ("vrr", "window", "滑", "freesync") + vrr_shown(s),
         ("network", {"ethernet": "ethernet", "wifi": "wifi"}.get(net, "offline"), "網", "network",
          s.get("network", "offline"), net in ("ethernet", "wifi") or s.get("network", "offline") != "offline")]
    if "bluetooth" in s:
        dev = s.get("devices", "off")
        t += [("bluetooth", "bluetooth", "青", "bluetooth", s["bluetooth"], s["bluetooth"] == "on"),
              ("devices", "headphones", "対", "devices", dev, dev not in ("none", "off"))]
    b = s.get("backup", "not set up")
    t += [("drives", "drive", "盤", "drives", s.get("drives", "none"), s.get("drives", "none") != "none"),
          ("backup", "archive", "蔵", "backup", b, b not in ("never", "not set up")),
          ("dnd", "bell_off" if s.get("dnd") == "on" else "bell", "静", "dnd", s.get("dnd", "off"), s.get("dnd") == "on"),
          ("saver", "hourglass", "夢", "screensaver", s.get("saver", "off"), s.get("saver", "off") != "off"),
          # the screen's two captures beside the foot's screenshot: a recording (its menu —
          # whole or a region, sound or none —, or stopped while one runs: stamped then) and
          # a region's words read onto the clipboard (kawa-screenshot text)
          ("record", "cassette_a", "録", "record", "recording" if s.get("record") == "on" else "off", s.get("record") == "on"),
          ("ocr", "note", "読", "read text", "a region", False)]
    # (each value as much as its tile shows: a gap each side, 104 px of words)
    return [(k, i, j, n, fit(v, TILE_W - 2 * GAP), on) for k, i, j, n, v, on in t]


def rows_of(n):
    """How many tiles each row holds: three a row; a short last row takes from the one above,
    so no row is ever a lone tile (7 → 3 2 2; 8 → 3 3 2)."""
    rows = [3] * (n // 3) + ([n % 3] if n % 3 else [])
    if len(rows) > 1 and rows[-1] == 1:
        rows[-2] -= 1
        rows[-1] += 1
    return rows


class Layout:
    """Every item's place (in the panel's own pixels), the content's top left, the rules'
    heights, the headings (kanji, name, x, y[, value]) and the empty lists' "none" (x, y), the
    size; a menu's too: what is drawn but not chosen (marks: its title, a disabled row, a
    grid's cells, the filter's line) and the part of it that scrolls (view: its top and
    height, and how tall what scrolls in it is — None: nothing scrolls)."""

    def __init__(self, items, head, rules, size, heads=(), nones=(), marks=(), view=None):
        self.items, self.head, self.rules, self.size = items, head, rules, size
        self.heads, self.nones, self.marks, self.view = list(heads), list(nones), list(marks), view


# ── a menu (kawa-panel --menu, kawa-menu's): a list read from its caller ──────────────────
# One line a thing, its fields apart by tabs; the line's number (from 0) is what --index
# prints, so a caller's own list of what each line does stays its own:
#     part  KANJI  NAME [VALUE]     a part's heading: its seal, its name, the stitch to the
#                                   right edge (a value at its end, quiet: "up 03h 07m")
#     row   GLYPH  NAME [DETAIL [VALUE]]
#                                   a choice: its glyph (an icon, ▣ □ — any character), its
#                                   name, a detail in a column of its own (every row's starts
#                                   at the same place, after the longest name), its value
#                                   right-aligned, quiet (an empty field: none)
#     on    GLYPH  NAME [DETAIL [VALUE]]   the same, the one in use (glyph and name strong)
#     off   GLYPH  NAME [DETAIL [VALUE]]   the same, not to be chosen now (faint: the cursor
#                                   and the pointer pass it)
#     info  GLYPH  NAME [DETAIL [VALUE]]   the same in its usual inks, nothing to choose: what
#                                   is only read (a day of the week's weather) — the cursor
#                                   and the pointer pass it
#     key   GLYPH  NAME             a key button; keys one after another share a row
#     cell  TEXT   [STATE]          a grid's cell, seven a row (a month): STATE today (stamped,
#                                   as a lit seal), dim (another month's day), head (a weekday)
#     note  TEXT                    a quiet line
#     (an empty line)               nothing (it keeps the numbering)
#     any other line                a choice of that text (a plain menu's line, as dmenu's)
# Printed: the chosen line's number (--index), else its name (with --prompt, Enter on what
# matches nothing: what was typed). Exit 0 chosen, 1 dismissed (Esc, a click outside, its key
# again, another menu or panel), 2 wrong use.
LINE_KINDS = ("part", "row", "on", "off", "info", "key", "cell", "note")
ROW_KINDS = ("row", "on", "off", "info")
SHOWN_KINDS = ("off", "info")       # rows drawn but never chosen
CELL_GAP = 4                         # seven cells a row: 7 × 50 + 6 × 4 = 374, within the content
MENU_MAX_H = 1080 - 31 - 8 - 8       # (a list taller than the screen under the bar: it scrolls)


def parse_line(i, line):
    """A line of a menu → (number, kind, fields): kind one of LINE_KINDS, plain (a choice of
    the whole line) or blank."""
    if not line.strip():
        return i, "blank", []
    f = line.split("\t")
    if f[0] in LINE_KINDS and len(f) >= 2:
        return i, f[0], f[1:]
    return i, "plain", [line]


class Menu:
    """A menu as asked: its lines and options (drop, title, width in columns, lines shown at
    most, the line the first key puts the cursor on, a prompt — typing filters —, index), and
    what has been typed."""

    def __init__(self, lines, drop=False, title="", width=0, lines_shown=0, select=None,
                 prompt=None, index=False, stay=False, password=False):
        self.lines = [parse_line(i, l) for i, l in enumerate(lines)]
        self.stay = stay          # (its keys answer and keep it on screen: the next page in place)
        self.drop, self.title, self.width, self.shown = drop, title, width, lines_shown
        self.select, self.prompt, self.index = select, prompt, index
        self.password = password  # (what is typed shown as ◆ each, never itself)
        self.query = ""

    def inner(self):
        """The content's width: as wide as its widest line needs (a row: a gap, its glyph, a
        gap, the name — the details' column after the longest name, two gaps on —, the values'
        column two gaps after the widest words, a gap at the end: columns(); a heading with its
        value; keys sharing a row, each as wide as the widest; a grid's whole content), or the
        columns asked for if wider. (Widths in the font's columns: 8 px, a wide letter 16.)"""
        rows = [f for _, k, f in self.lines if k in ROW_KINDS]
        dx, vright = columns(rows, px_of)
        need = [GAP + px_of(self.title) + GAP if self.title else 0, vright + GAP if vright else 0]
        run = []                                  # (keys one after another: equal, sharing a row)
        for _, k, f in self.lines + [(None, "end", [])]:
            if k == "key":
                run.append(2 * GAP + LINE + GAP + px_of(fld(f, 1)))
                continue
            if run:
                need.append(len(run) * max(run) + (len(run) - 1) * GAP)
                run = []
            if k in ROW_KINDS:
                if not fld(f, 3):
                    need.append((dx + px_of(fld(f, 2)) if fld(f, 2) else NAME_X + px_of(fld(f, 1))) + GAP)
            elif k == "plain":
                need.append(NAME_X + px_of(f[0]) + GAP)
            elif k == "part":
                need.append(SEAL + GAP + px_of(fld(f, 1)) + GAP + 4 * GAP + (GAP + px_of(fld(f, 2)) if fld(f, 2) else 0))
            elif k == "cell":
                need.append(INNER)
            elif k == "note":
                need.append(GAP + px_of(f[0]) + GAP)
        return max(need + [COL * self.width + 2 * GAP if self.width else 0, 160])

    def matches(self, *words):
        """A choice the typed words all find (in its name, detail or value, in any case)."""
        hay = " ".join(words).lower()
        return all(w in hay for w in self.query.lower().split())

    def name_of(self, i):
        _, kind, f = self.lines[i]
        return f[0] if kind == "plain" else (f[1] if len(f) > 1 and f[1] else f[0])


def px_of(t):
    """Its width in the font: a column a letter, two a wide one (a kanji, a kana, a full-width
    sign: East Asian width W or F — Sumi UI draws exactly that, tools/build-fonts.py)."""
    return sum(2 * COL if unicodedata.east_asian_width(c) in "WF" else COL for c in t)


# a meter's slider ends this far from its right edge, in quick settings and the sound panel
# alike: they start two gaps after the longest name ("brightness"), under one another
TRAIL = INNER - (NAME_X + px_of("brightness") + 2 * GAP) - SPAN


def fit(t, w, tail=False):
    """t cut to w px — at a word's end where one is (a single word too long loses its last
    letters); tail: its first letters go, the end of what is typed shows. Never an "…": a
    tile's name (a Wi-Fi's, a drive's, a device's) shows what fits of it."""
    if px_of(t) <= w:
        return t
    cut = t
    while cut and px_of(cut) > w:
        cut = cut[1:] if tail else cut[:-1]
    if not tail and not cut.endswith(" ") and " " in cut.strip() and t[len(cut):len(cut) + 1] != " ":
        cut = cut.rsplit(" ", 1)[0]          # (a cut that ends on a space ends at a word already)
    return cut.strip()


def fld(f, i):
    return f[i] if len(f) > i else ""


def columns(rows, width):
    """A menu's rows (fields GLYPH NAME DETAIL VALUE) → where its columns go, from the row's
    left edge: the details' (two gaps after the longest name of a row with a detail; its
    glyph a gap in, the names at NAME_X) and the values' right edge — two gaps after the
    widest text of a row with a value, plus the widest value: the values right-aligned in a
    column of their own, against the words, not the menu's far edge (0: no values).
    width(TEXT) → px."""
    named = [width(fld(f, 1)) for f in rows if fld(f, 2)]
    dx = NAME_X + (max(named) if named else 0) + 2 * GAP
    valued = [f for f in rows if fld(f, 3)]
    if not valued:
        return dx, 0
    ends = [dx + width(fld(f, 2)) if fld(f, 2) else NAME_X + width(fld(f, 1)) for f in valued]
    return dx, max(ends) + 2 * GAP + max(width(fld(f, 3)) for f in valued)


def layout_menu(m):
    """A menu's place for each thing, as the sound panel's: parts under seals (SECTION between
    parts), rows of METER_H 2 px apart, keys KEY_H high sharing a row a gap apart, a grid's cells.
    While something is typed only the choices it finds show, under their parts."""
    x0 = BORDER + PAD
    w = m.inner()
    y = x0
    items, heads, marks, r = [], [], [], 0
    if m.title:
        marks.append(("note", x0, y, w, m.title))
        y += METER_H + GAP
    if m.prompt is not None:
        marks.append(("input", x0, y, w, KEY_H, m.prompt, "◆" * len(m.query) if m.password else m.query))
        # (the line typed on, then the room of a part's break before the first row — as in
        # every text screen, one blank line between where you type and what it finds)
        y += KEY_H + SECTION
    top = y
    lines = m.lines
    if m.query:                                   # (typing: the choices it finds, under their parts)
        keep, part = [], None
        for ln in lines:
            if ln[1] == "part":
                part = ln
            elif ln[1] in ("row", "on", "info", "plain") and m.matches(*ln[2][1:]):
                if part is not None:
                    keep.append(part)
                    part = None
                keep.append(ln)
        lines = keep
    prev = None                                   # (what came before: the space before this)
    k = 0
    while k < len(lines):
        i, kind, f = lines[k]
        if kind == "blank":
            k += 1
            continue
        if kind == "part":
            if prev is not None:
                y += SECTION - (METER_GAP if prev in ("row", "cell") else 0)
            heads.append((f[0], f[1] if len(f) > 1 else "", x0, y) + ((f[2],) if len(f) > 2 and f[2] else ()))
            y += SEAL + GAP
            prev = None
        elif kind == "key":
            run = []
            while k < len(lines) and lines[k][1] == "key":
                run.append(lines[k])
                k += 1
            k -= 1
            if prev in ("row", "cell", "note"):
                y += GAP - METER_GAP
            elif prev == "key":
                y += GAP
            n = len(run)
            kw = (w - (n - 1) * GAP) // n
            for c, (j, _, g) in enumerate(run):
                xx = x0 + c * (kw + GAP)
                items.append(Item(str(j), "key", xx, y, (w - xx + x0) if c == n - 1 else kw, KEY_H, r,
                                  glyph=g[0], name=g[1] if len(g) > 1 else ""))
            y += KEY_H
            r += 1
            prev = "key"
        elif kind == "cell":
            run = []
            while k < len(lines) and lines[k][1] == "cell":
                run.append(lines[k])
                k += 1
            k -= 1
            if prev == "key":
                y += GAP
            cw = (w - 6 * CELL_GAP) // 7
            for c, (j, _, g) in enumerate(run):
                if c and c % 7 == 0:
                    y += METER_H + METER_GAP
                xx = x0 + (c % 7) * (cw + CELL_GAP)
                marks.append(("cell", xx, y, cw, METER_H, g[0], g[1] if len(g) > 1 else ""))
            y += METER_H + METER_GAP
            prev = "cell"
        else:                                     # a row (row, on, off, plain) or a note
            if prev == "key":
                y += GAP
            if kind == "note":
                marks.append(("note", x0, y, w, f[0]))
            elif kind in SHOWN_KINDS:
                marks.append((kind, x0, y, w, f[0], fld(f, 1), fld(f, 2), fld(f, 3)))
            elif kind == "plain":
                items.append(Item(str(i), "row", x0, y, w, METER_H, r, glyph="", name=f[0]))
                r += 1
            else:
                items.append(Item(str(i), "row", x0, y, w, METER_H, r, glyph=f[0], name=fld(f, 1),
                                  detail=fld(f, 2), value=fld(f, 3), lit=kind == "on"))
                r += 1
            y += METER_H + METER_GAP
            prev = "row" if kind != "note" else "note"
        k += 1
    if prev in ("row", "cell", "note"):
        y -= METER_GAP
    if m.query and not items and not m.password and any(k in ROW_KINDS + ("plain",) for _, k, _ in m.lines):
        marks.append(("none", x0, y))             # (nothing found: Enter gives what was typed)
        y += METER_H
    height = y - top
    view_h = min(height, MENU_MAX_H - top - PAD - BORDER)
    # (each line's foot — a choice's, or a row only read: keys share one)
    ends = sorted({it.y + it.h for it in items} | {mk[2] + METER_H for mk in marks if mk[0] in SHOWN_KINDS})
    if m.shown and len(ends) > m.shown:          # (at most N lines of choices, whole: the view
        view_h = min(view_h, ends[m.shown - 1] - top)   # ends under the Nth, its parts above it)
    for it in items:
        it.scrolls = True
    return Layout(items, (x0, x0), [], (w + 2 * (PAD + BORDER), top + view_h + PAD + BORDER), heads,
                  [], marks, (top, view_h, height) if view_h < height else None)


def layout_quick(s):
    """設 quick settings under its seal, as every panel opens; the two meters; the tiles (three
    a row, a short last row two wide ones: 184 × 80); the keys at the foot, the whole width."""
    x0 = y = BORDER + PAD
    items, rules, r = [], [], 0
    head = (x0, y)
    heads = [("設", "quick settings", x0, y)]
    y += SEAL + GAP
    for key, kanji, name in (("brightness", "明", "brightness"), ("sound", "音", "sound")):
        items.append(Item(key, "meter", x0, y, INNER, METER_H, r, kanji=kanji, name=name, value=s.get(key, "")))
        y += METER_H + METER_GAP
        r += 1
    y += RULE - METER_GAP
    rules.append(y - RULE + RULE // 2)
    t = tiles_of(s)
    i = 0
    for n in rows_of(len(t)):
        w = (INNER - (n - 1) * GAP) // n
        for c in range(n):
            key, ic, kanji, name, value, lit = t[i]
            items.append(Item(key, "tile", x0 + c * (w + GAP), y, w, TILE_H, r, icon=ic, kanji=kanji,
                              name=name, value=value, lit=lit))
            i += 1
        y += TILE_H + GAP
        r += 1
    y += RULE - GAP
    rules.append(y - RULE + RULE // 2)
    # (the keys the whole width, a gap apart: power its icon alone, the two others sharing the rest)
    half = (INNER - KEY_H - 2 * GAP) // 2
    keys = [("wallpaper", "picture", "wallpaper", half), ("screenshot", "camera", "screenshot", INNER - KEY_H - 2 * GAP - half),
            ("power", "power", "", KEY_H)]
    x = x0
    for key, ic, name, w in keys:
        items.append(Item(key, "key", x, y, w, KEY_H, r, icon=ic, name=name,
                          tip=s.get("wallpaper", "") if key == "wallpaper" else ""))
        x += w + GAP
    y += KEY_H + PAD + BORDER
    return Layout(items, head, rules, (INNER + 2 * (PAD + BORDER), y), heads)


def layout_sound(s):
    """音 sound: the volume's meter, then mute and mixer side by side · 出 output and 入 input:
    a row per device (a list with none says "none") · mute microphone at the input's foot.
    Each part under its seal; a seal's stitch runs to the right edge."""
    x0 = y = BORDER + PAD
    items, heads, nones, r = [], [], [], 0
    muted = s.get("muted") == "on"
    heads.append(("音", "sound", x0, y))
    y += SEAL + GAP
    items.append(Item("sound", "meter", x0, y, INNER, METER_H, r, name="volume", value=s.get("sound", ""),
                      muted=muted))
    y += METER_H + GAP
    r += 1
    half = (INNER - GAP) // 2
    items.append(Item("mute", "key", x0, y, half, KEY_H, r, icon="speaker" if muted else "speaker_off",
                      name="unmute" if muted else "mute"))
    items.append(Item("mixer", "key", x0 + half + GAP, y, INNER - half - GAP, KEY_H, r, icon="sliders", name="mixer"))
    y += KEY_H
    r += 1
    for kanji, name, pre, devs in (("出", "output", "out", s.get("outputs", [])),
                                   ("入", "input", "in", s.get("inputs", []))):
        y += SECTION
        heads.append((kanji, name, x0, y))
        y += SEAL + GAP
        if not devs:
            nones.append((x0, y))
            y += METER_H + METER_GAP
        for dev_id, default, label in devs:
            items.append(Item(f"{pre}:{dev_id}", "row", x0, y, INNER, METER_H, r, icon="box_on" if default else "box_off",
                              name=label, lit=default))
            y += METER_H + METER_GAP
            r += 1
        y -= METER_GAP
    if s.get("mic", "none") != "none":                # (no input: no microphone to mute)
        mic_muted = s.get("mic") == "muted"
        clean = s.get("cleanmic", "none")
        y += GAP
        if clean == "on":                             # (the clean mic's level, live: it only shows)
            items.append(Item("level", "level", x0, y, INNER, METER_H, r, icon="mic", name="clean mic",
                              value=s.get("level", "0%")))
            y += METER_H + GAP
            r += 1
        items.append(Item("mic", "key", x0, y, INNER, KEY_H, r, icon="mic" if mic_muted else "mic_off",
                          name="unmute microphone" if mic_muted else "mute microphone"))
        y += KEY_H + GAP
        r += 1
        if clean != "none":                           # (not loaded: the test alone, the row's width)
            items.append(Item("cleanmic", "key", x0, y, half, KEY_H, r, icon="box_on" if clean == "on" else "box_off",
                              name="clean mic", lit=clean == "on"))
        tx = x0 if clean == "none" else x0 + half + GAP
        items.append(Item("mictest", "key", tx, y, x0 + INNER - tx, KEY_H, r, icon="mic", name="test mic"))
        y += KEY_H
    y += PAD + BORDER
    return Layout(items, (x0, x0), [], (INNER + 2 * (PAD + BORDER), y), heads, nones)


def layout_brightness(s):
    """The brightness meter alone, the size of what it holds: its kanji and name, two gaps,
    the slider — the kanji's ink and the slider's end on the content's edges (the
    slider as quick settings draws it)."""
    x0 = BORDER + PAD
    w = LINE + GAP + px_of("brightness") + 2 * GAP + SPAN
    it = Item("brightness", "meter", x0, x0, w, METER_H, 0, kanji="明", name="brightness",
              value=s.get("brightness", ""), inset=0, trail=0)
    return Layout([it], (x0, x0), [], (w + 2 * (PAD + BORDER), x0 + METER_H + PAD + BORDER))


def layout(s, mode="quick"):
    if mode == "menu" and isinstance(s, Launcher):
        return layout_launcher(s)
    return {"quick": layout_quick, "sound": layout_sound, "brightness": layout_brightness,
            "menu": layout_menu}[mode](s)


# ── the launcher (the torii, Super+Space) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A menu of its own kind: under its seal (起 launcher) the line you type on, then — while what
# you typed is a sum, a percentage or a conversion — its answer, worked out as you type (qalc,
# one for the session's panel: the calculator's own), then the programs it finds, best first:
# a name that starts with it, a word of the name that does, a name that holds it, what the
# program is or its keywords (GenericName, Keywords: "browser" finds Brave), the letters in
# order; equals, the one started more often first, then by name. Ten rows show; more scroll.
# Each its icon (22 × 22, shown 1:1): a window program's own (the icon theme's, hicolor), or
# a seal of ours — the entry's kanji (X-Fedokawaii-Kanji) in a 1 px frame, as a heading's —
# for a program in a terminal or one with none. Enter (or a click) starts it — the answer's
# row copies the answer (a notice says so, with it) — and text that finds nothing runs as a
# command when its first word is one. The same key or the torii again closes it, as every
# panel; it opens in the middle of the screen, its size fixed while you type.
APP_ICON = 22                        # an entry's icon: the 24 px row less a pixel above and below
LAUNCH_ROWS = 10


class App:
    def __init__(self, aid, name, generic, words, kanji, info):
        self.id, self.name, self.info, self.kanji = aid, name, info, kanji
        self.low = name.lower()
        self.words = self.low.split()
        self.more = " ".join([generic or ""] + list(words or [])).lower()
        self.pic = None              # its icon, once loaded (a pixbuf), or the seal's kanji


def rank(app, words):
    """How well an entry matches the typed words (each must match somewhere): 0 best … 4
    (the letters in order in its name), None: not at all."""
    worst = 0
    for w in words:
        if app.low.startswith(w):
            t = 0
        elif any(x.startswith(w) for x in app.words):
            t = 1
        elif w in app.low:
            t = 2
        elif w in app.more or w in app.id.lower():
            t = 3
        else:
            it = iter(app.low)
            if not all(c in it for c in w):
                return None
            t = 4
        worst = max(worst, t)
    return worst


class Launcher(Menu):
    """The launcher's menu: the entries (App), how often each was started, what is typed, the
    answer to it when it is a sum (calc), the entries shown (found)."""

    def __init__(self, apps, counts):
        Menu.__init__(self, [], prompt="")
        self.apps, self.counts = apps, counts
        self.calc = None                     # (the typed text, its answer)
        self.found = []
        self.refind()

    def refind(self):
        words = self.query.lower().split()
        got = []
        for a in self.apps:
            r = rank(a, words) if words else 0
            if r is not None:
                got.append((r, -self.counts.get(a.id, 0), a.low, a))
        got.sort(key=lambda t: t[:3])
        self.found = [t[3] for t in got]

    def answer_now(self):
        """The answer's row, when it answers what is typed now."""
        return self.calc[1] if self.calc and self.calc[0] == self.query else None


def layout_launcher(m):
    x0 = BORDER + PAD
    w = INNER
    y = x0
    heads = [("起", "launcher", x0, y)]
    y += SEAL + GAP
    marks = [("input", x0, y, w, KEY_H, "", m.query)]
    y += KEY_H + SECTION
    top = y
    items, r = [], 0
    ans = m.answer_now()
    rows = ([("calc", None)] if ans else []) + [(f"a{k}", a) for k, a in enumerate(m.found)]
    for key, a in rows:
        it = Item(key, "app", x0, y, w, METER_H, r, app=a,
                  name=ans if a is None else a.name, value="copy" if a is None else "")
        it.scrolls = True
        items.append(it)
        y += METER_H + METER_GAP
        r += 1
    if rows:
        y -= METER_GAP
    elif m.query:
        marks.append(("none", x0, y))        # (Enter: what was typed runs, its first word a program)
        y += METER_H
    height = y - top
    view_h = LAUNCH_ROWS * (METER_H + METER_GAP) - METER_GAP
    lay = Layout(items, (x0, x0), [], (w + 2 * (PAD + BORDER), top + view_h + PAD + BORDER), heads,
                 [], marks, (top, view_h, max(height, view_h)) if height > view_h else (top, view_h, view_h))
    lay.fixed = True                         # (its size kept while typing: never trimmed to its ink)
    return lay


def look(it, focused, hot):
    """How an item is drawn — the colours' names: fill (None: none of its own), frame (None:
    none), glyph (its icon, or a meter's kanji), label (its name), value (its words, a meter's
    words with no level), on_fill (its seal and slider drawn as on vermilion), detail (a menu
    row's second column: text, its ink on the cursor's fill).
    The keyboard's cursor (focused) is filled in vermilion, its ink text_on_accent. The pointer
    (hot) draws no box: an item under it keeps its resting look and only its glyph and its name
    turn accent_text; under the pointer and the cursor both, it is drawn as the cursor (ink on
    vermilion keeps its contrast).
    A tile's seal says whether the thing is on, so the pointer leaves it as it is. A device's
    row: the one in use ticked and in strong ink, the others' box quiet."""
    if focused:
        ink = "text_on_accent"
        return {"fill": "accent", "frame": None, "glyph": ink, "label": ink,
                "value": ink, "on_fill": True, "detail": ink}
    bare = it.kind in ("meter", "level", "row", "app")
    glyph = {"meter": "accent_text", "level": "accent_text", "row": "strong" if it.lit else "muted"}.get(it.kind, "text")
    rest = {"fill": None if bare else "key", "frame": None if bare else "edge", "glyph": glyph,
            "label": "strong" if it.kind in ("tile", "row") and it.lit else "text",
            "value": "muted", "on_fill": False, "detail": "text"}
    if hot:
        rest["glyph"] = rest["label"] = "accent_text"
    return rest


SWITCHES = ("theme", "night", "vrr", "bluetooth", "dnd", "saver")   # done in place: the panel stays
WHEELED = {"night": ("warmer", "cooler")}   # a tile the wheel changes: the night light's warmth
OPENERS = ("network", "devices", "drives", "backup", "wallpaper", "screenshot", "record", "ocr", "power")
METERS = ("brightness", "sound")
MOVES = ("Left", "h", "Right", "l", "Up", "k", "Down", "j", "Tab", "ISO_Left_Tab")
ENTER = ("Return", "KP_Enter", "space")


class Panel:
    """Everything but the drawing: which panel is open, where the cursor is, what a key or a
    click does. The window, the processes and the clock are the ui's (GTK's in main())."""

    def __init__(self, ui):
        self.ui = ui
        self.mode = "quick"      # the panel open (or the last one open)
        self.states = {m: {} for m in MODES}
        self.states["menu"] = Menu([])
        self.reply = None        # a menu's answer to its caller (rc, text), until it is given
        self.holding = False     # a --stay menu's key answered: on screen, waiting for its next page
        self.queued = []         # keys pressed meanwhile: each one done on the page as it comes
        self.scroll = 0          # how far a menu's list is scrolled (px)
        self.relayout()
        self.focus = None        # the keyboard's cursor (filled in vermilion): none until a key
        self.hot = None          # the item under the pointer: its glyph and name lit, no box
        self.pointer = None      # where the pointer is over the panel (None: not over it)
        self.dragging = None     # the meter being dragged
        self.visible = False
        self.dy, self.win = 0, self.size
        self.place = where(None, self.size[0])
        self.what = None         # what is open (or was last): a panel's mode, or "menu CALLER"
        self.used = None         # the last bar click a request came from (its id: ui.click())
        self.lost = None         # (what, click): closed by a press on the bar, not yet asked for
        self.reading = set()     # the modes whose state is being read
        self.again = set()       # … and asked again meanwhile
        self.busy = {}           # a meter's level being set, and the next one asked meanwhile
        self.wanted = {}
        # a level set here is the truth until confirmed: a state read started before its last
        # change (or answered while it is dragged or being set) does not move it back
        self.seq = 0             # counts the meters' changes
        self.changed = {}        # meter → self.seq at its last change
        self.read_seq = {}       # mode → self.seq when its state read under way started
        self.read_hold = {}      # mode → the meters being dragged or set when it started
        self.scrolled = (None, 0.0)   # a touchpad's small scrolls, summed per meter

    @property
    def state(self):
        return self.states[self.mode]

    def relayout(self):
        lay = layout(self.state, self.mode)
        self.items, self.head, self.rules, self.size = lay.items, lay.head, lay.rules, lay.size
        self.heads, self.nones, self.marks, self.view = lay.heads, lay.nones, lay.marks, lay.view
        if self.view:
            self.scroll = max(0, min(self.scroll, self.view[2] - self.view[1]))
        else:
            self.scroll = 0
        # the pads above and below measured to the ink (design-system §3): what is drawn is
        # looked at, and the window starts dy px lower and is h high, 12 px of paper between
        # the frame and the first and the last ink
        if getattr(lay, "fixed", False):
            self.dy, self.win = 0, self.size
        else:
            self.dy, h, w = self.ui.trim(self.size)
            self.win = (w, h)

    # the state
    def refresh(self, mode=None):
        mode = mode or self.mode
        if mode in self.reading:
            self.again.add(mode)
            return
        self.reading.add(mode)
        self.read_seq[mode] = self.seq
        self.read_hold[mode] = {k for k in METERS if self.setting(k)}
        self.ui.run(["kawa-quick", "--state"] + ([] if mode == "quick" else [mode]),
                    lambda rc, out: self.got_state(mode, rc, out))

    def setting(self, key):
        """Dragged, or a level of it being set or waiting to be."""
        return self.dragging == key or key in self.wanted or key in self.busy

    def held(self, key, mode="quick"):
        """A meter whose level here outranks a state read (of mode) now coming back: dragged,
        being set, or changed after that read started, or dragged or being set when it started
        (it would show the level from before)."""
        return (self.setting(key) or self.changed.get(key, -1) > self.read_seq.get(mode, -1)
                or key in self.read_hold.get(mode, ()))

    def got_state(self, mode, rc, out):
        self.reading.discard(mode)
        if rc == 0:
            new = parse_sound(out) if mode == "sound" else parse_state(out)
            old = self.states[mode]
            for k in METERS:
                if k in old and self.held(k, mode):
                    new[k] = old[k]
                    if k == "sound" and "muted" in old:
                        new["muted"] = old["muted"]
            if "level" in old:                        # (the clean mic's level: the listener's, live)
                new["level"] = old["level"]
            self.states[mode] = new
            self.listen()
            if mode == self.mode:
                self.relayout()
                keys = [i.key for i in self.items]
                if self.focus not in keys:
                    self.focus = None
                self.rehot()
                self.ui.redraw()
        if mode in self.again:
            self.again.discard(mode)
            self.refresh(mode)

    # open, closed
    def request(self, what="quick", menu=None, reply=None):
        """A request (its key, or its button on the bar): what — a panel's mode, or "menu
        CALLER" (menu: the Menu, reply(rc, text) its caller's answer) — opens, or closes if it
        is the one open. A click on the bar that closed it (a click outside it) is that close:
        it stays closed. Every request answered at once: a menu's caller hears 1 unless it opens."""
        click = self.ui.click()
        if click is not None and click == self.used:
            click = None                              # (that click was answered: this is a key)
        if click is not None:
            self.used = click
        if self.visible and self.what == what and menu is not None and self.holding:
            self.page(menu, reply)                    # (its next page: in place, at once)
        elif self.visible and self.what == what:
            self._refuse(reply)
            self.close("toggled")
        elif click is not None and self.lost == (what, click):
            self.lost = None                          # (the click outside it was on its own button)
            self._refuse(reply)
        elif menu is not None:
            self.open_menu(menu, reply, what)
        else:
            self.open(what)

    def page(self, menu, reply):
        """A --stay menu's next page (its caller asked again after one of its keys): the lines
        replaced in the same window — no close, no new window, the cursor on the key that paged
        (its --select) and the pointer's item lit as it was; a key pressed while it was on its
        way is done now, on this page (none is lost, however fast the clicks)."""
        self.holding = False
        self.states["menu"] = menu
        self.reply = reply
        self.scroll = 0
        self.relayout()
        keys = [i.key for i in self.items]
        sel = str(menu.select) if menu.select is not None else None
        self.focus = sel if sel in keys else (self.focus if self.focus in keys else (keys[0] if keys else None))
        self.rehot()
        self.ui.redraw()
        if self.queued:
            self.activate(self.queued.pop(0))

    def _refuse(self, reply):
        if reply:
            reply(1, "")

    def open(self, mode="quick"):
        if self.visible:
            self.close("switched")                    # (one panel or menu at a time)
        self.mode = self.what = mode
        self.lost = None
        self.visible = True
        # a new window, nothing remembered: no cursor, nothing hot until the pointer says
        # (it says at once if it is over the panel: enter-notify), no drag
        self.focus = self.hot = self.pointer = self.dragging = None
        self.scrolled = (None, 0.0)
        self.relayout()
        self.place = where(self.ui.clicked(), self.size[0])   # (under the click, or its key's place)
        self.ui.show()
        self.ui.light(mode, True)
        self.refresh()

    def open_menu(self, menu, reply, what="menu"):
        """A menu asked for (kawa-menu): shown at once in place of whatever panel or menu is on
        screen (that one dismissed); reply(rc, text) answers its caller once."""
        if self.visible:
            self.close("switched")
        self.mode, self.what = "menu", what
        self.lost = None
        self.states["menu"] = menu
        self.reply = reply
        self.visible = True
        self.focus = self.hot = self.pointer = self.dragging = None
        self.scrolled = (None, 0.0)
        self.scroll = 0
        self.holding, self.queued = False, []
        self.relayout()
        # keyboard-first: the cursor on its first choice (or --select's line) from the start —
        # Enter takes it at once; the pointer moves it (hover(): one cursor, never two lights)
        self.focus = self.first_key() if self.items else None
        self.place = menu_place(menu.drop, self.ui.clicked() if menu.drop else None, self.size[0])
        self.ui.show()
        if menu.drop or what == "launcher":
            self.ui.light(what, True)                 # (its button on the bar lit)

    def answer(self, rc, text=""):
        """The menu's caller told, once."""
        reply, self.reply = self.reply, None
        if reply:
            reply(rc, text)

    def close(self, how):
        if not self.visible:
            return
        self.visible = False
        self.holding, self.queued = False, []
        if how == "lost":
            # a click outside it: on the bar, a click not yet asked for — the request it makes
            # (if it is this one's button) is this close
            click = self.ui.click()
            if click is not None and click != self.used:
                self.lost = (self.what, click)
        self.focus = self.hot = self.pointer = self.dragging = None
        self.listen()                                 # (the clean mic's meter: no one reading it now)
        try:
            self.ui.hide()
        finally:                                      # (whatever else fails:)
            if self.mode == "menu":
                self.answer(1)                        # a menu dismissed, unless it was answered
                if self.states["menu"].drop or self.what == "launcher":
                    self.ui.light(self.what, False)
            else:
                self.ui.light(self.mode, False)       # a panel's marker gone

    # the clean mic's level: read (ui.listen) only while the sound panel is open and shows it
    def listen(self):
        self.ui.listen(self.visible and self.mode == "sound" and self.states["sound"].get("cleanmic") == "on")

    def heard(self, pct):
        """The listener's newest level: kept, and its meter alone redrawn."""
        self.states["sound"]["level"] = f"{pct}%"
        it = self.item("level") if self.mode == "sound" else None
        if it:
            it.value = self.states["sound"]["level"]
            self.ui.redraw(it)

    # doing
    def item(self, key):
        return next((i for i in self.items if i.key == key), None)

    def activate(self, key):
        if self.mode == "menu" and isinstance(self.state, Launcher):
            it = self.item(key)
            if it is None:
                return
            self.close("chosen")
            if it.app is None:
                self.ui.copy_answer(it.name)
            else:
                self.ui.start(it.app)
            return
        if self.mode == "menu":
            m = self.state
            it = self.item(key)
            if it is None:
                return
            if m.stay and it.kind == "key":
                if self.holding:                      # (the next page not here yet: done on it)
                    self.queued.append(key)
                    return
                self.holding = True
                self.answer(0, key if m.index else m.name_of(int(key)))
                # (its caller gone or silent: closed after a second, never left hanging)
                self.ui.later(1000, lambda: self.holding and self.close("chosen"))
                return
            self.answer(0, key if m.index else m.name_of(int(key)))
            self.close("chosen")
            return
        if self.mode == "quick":
            if key in SWITCHES:
                self.ui.run(["kawa-quick", "--do", key], lambda rc, out: self.refresh())
            elif key == "sound":
                self.open("sound")                    # (its own panel: the outputs, the inputs)
            elif key in OPENERS:
                self.close("chosen")
                self.ui.launch(["kawa-quick", "--do", key])
        elif self.mode == "sound":
            it = self.item(key)
            if key in ("mute", "mic"):
                self.ui.run(["kawa-quick", "--do", "mute" if key == "mute" else "micmute"],
                            lambda rc, out: self.refresh())
            elif key == "cleanmic":                   # (stays open: the key and the ticks show the new state)
                self.ui.run(["kawa-quick", "--do", "cleanmic"], lambda rc, out: self.refresh())
            elif key == "mictest":                    # (stays open: the level shows while it records)
                self.ui.launch(["kawa-quick", "--do", "mictest"])
            elif key == "mixer":
                self.close("chosen")
                self.ui.launch(["kawa-quick", "--do", "mixer"])
            elif it and it.kind == "row" and not it.lit:   # (the one in use: nothing to change)
                pre, dev = key.split(":", 1)
                self.ui.run(["kawa-quick", "--do", "output" if pre == "out" else "input", dev],
                            lambda rc, out: self.refresh())
        # (a meter's Enter elsewhere: the meter is all there is)

    def set_level(self, key, pct):
        """The meter at pct at once (only it redrawn); the level sent when none is being set,
        else the newest asked goes when that one is done. The same level again does nothing
        (but unmutes a muted sound: a level set is a level heard)."""
        pct = max(0, min(100, int(pct)))
        muted = key == "sound" and self.state.get("muted") == "on"
        if percent(self.state.get(key, "")) == pct and not muted:
            return
        self.seq += 1
        self.changed[key] = self.seq
        self.wanted[key] = pct
        for st in (self.states[m] for m in MODES):    # (every panel shows it: the same level)
            if key in st:
                st[key] = f"{pct}%"
        if key == "sound" and "muted" in self.states["sound"]:
            self.states["sound"]["muted"] = "off"     # (kawa-quick --do volume unmutes)
        it = self.item(key)
        if it:
            it.value, it.muted = self.state[key], False
        self.ui.redraw(*([it] if it else []))
        # the bar's module the same moment, before the level is sent (that takes a process):
        # the bar, the meter and the level display agree at every step
        self.ui.push("volume" if key == "sound" else key, f"volume {pct} 0" if key == "sound" else str(pct))
        if key not in self.busy:
            self._send(key)

    def _send(self, key):
        pct = self.wanted.get(key)
        if pct is None:
            return
        self.busy[key] = pct
        do = "brightness" if key == "brightness" else "volume"
        self.ui.run(["kawa-quick", "--do", do, str(pct)], lambda rc, out, k=key: self._sent(k))

    def _sent(self, key):
        sent = self.busy.pop(key, None)
        if key in self.wanted and self.wanted[key] != sent:
            self._send(key)                           # (moved again meanwhile: the latest)
        else:
            self.wanted.pop(key, None)
            self.refresh()

    def step(self, key, d):
        now = percent(self.state.get(key, ""))
        if now is not None:
            self.set_level(key, snap(now, d))

    @staticmethod
    def slider_x(item):
        """Where a meter's slider starts (as drawn)."""
        return item.x + item.w - getattr(item, "trail", TRAIL) - SPAN

    def meter_at(self, item, x, held=False):
        """The level a pointer at x on a meter means: on its slider (or a gap either side, the
        left margin meaning 0); held (a drag): anywhere, past an end that end. None: not there."""
        off = x - self.slider_x(item)
        if not held and not (-GAP <= off < SPAN + GAP // 2):
            return None
        return level_at(off)

    # keys
    def key(self, name, char=""):
        if name == "Escape":
            self.close("chosen")
            return
        if self.mode == "menu" and self.state.prompt is not None and self.typed(name, char):
            return
        cur = self.item(self.focus)
        if cur is None:
            # no cursor yet (every open starts without one): the first key puts it on the
            # first item — but a lone meter (the brightness slider) is all there is, so ← →
            # change it from the first press
            if len(self.items) == 1 and self.items[0].kind == "meter" and name in ("Left", "h", "Right", "l"):
                self.step(self.items[0].key, -5 if name in ("Left", "h") else 5)
            elif name in MOVES + ENTER and self.items:
                self.move(self.first_key())
            return
        if name in ENTER:
            self.activate(cur.key)
        elif name in ("Left", "h", "Right", "l") and cur.kind == "meter":
            self.step(cur.key, -5 if name in ("Left", "h") else 5)
        elif name in ("Left", "h", "Right", "l"):
            row = [i for i in self.items if i.row == cur.row]
            j = row.index(cur) + (-1 if name in ("Left", "h") else 1)
            if 0 <= j < len(row):
                self.move(row[j].key)
        elif name in ("Up", "k", "Down", "j"):
            r = cur.row + (-1 if name in ("Up", "k") else 1)
            row = [i for i in self.items if i.row == r]
            if row:
                self.move(min(row, key=lambda i: (abs(i.centre() - cur.centre()), i.x)).key)
        elif name in ("Tab", "ISO_Left_Tab"):
            keys = [i.key for i in self.items]
            j = (keys.index(cur.key) + (-1 if name == "ISO_Left_Tab" else 1)) % len(keys)
            self.move(keys[j])

    def first_key(self):
        """Where the first key puts the cursor: a menu's --select line, else the first item."""
        sel = self.state.select if self.mode == "menu" else None
        return str(sel) if sel is not None and self.item(str(sel)) else self.items[0].key

    def typed(self, name, char):
        """A menu with a prompt: a letter typed, or one taken back — the choices it finds
        shown, the cursor on the first (Enter takes it; on none, Enter gives what was typed).
        Whether the key was one of those."""
        m = self.state
        if name == "BackSpace":
            if not m.query:
                return True
            m.query = m.query[:-1]
        elif name in ("Return", "KP_Enter") and not self.items and m.query:
            if isinstance(m, Launcher):
                self.close("chosen")
                self.ui.command(m.query)              # (its first word a program: run)
                return True
            self.answer(0, m.query)
            self.close("chosen")
            return True
        elif char and char.isprintable() and name not in ("Return", "KP_Enter"):
            m.query += char                           # (a space too: "750 KB" is typed so)
        else:
            return False
        if isinstance(m, Launcher):
            m.refind()
            self.ui.calc(m)                           # (its answer, if it is a sum: comes when worked out)
        self.scroll = 0
        self.relayout()
        self.focus = self.items[0].key if self.items else None
        self.rehot()
        self.ui.redraw()
        return True

    def answered(self, m):
        """A sum's answer came (ui.calc): shown, when the launcher is still on screen with it."""
        if not (self.visible and self.mode == "menu" and self.state is m):
            return
        first = self.focus == (self.items[0].key if self.items else None)
        self.relayout()
        if first and self.items:
            self.focus = self.items[0].key            # (the cursor on the first row: the answer's now)
        self.rehot()
        self.ui.redraw()

    def move(self, key):
        if key != self.focus:
            old, self.focus = self.item(self.focus), key
            if self.view and self.seen(self.item(key)):
                self.ui.redraw()
                return
            self.ui.redraw(*[i for i in (old, self.item(key)) if i])

    def seen(self, it):
        """A menu's list scrolled so the item is whole in view; whether it had to move."""
        top, h, _ = self.view
        y0, y1 = it.y - self.scroll, it.y + it.h - self.scroll
        old = self.scroll
        if y0 < top:
            self.scroll -= top - y0
        elif y1 > top + h:
            self.scroll += y1 - (top + h)
        return self.scroll != old

    # the mouse
    def at(self, x, y):
        if self.view:                                 # (a menu's list that scrolls: only what shows)
            top, h, _ = self.view
            if not top <= y < top + h:
                return None
        return next((i for i in self.items if i.inside(x, y + (self.scroll if i.scrolls else 0))), None)

    def hover(self, x, y, dragging=False):
        """The pointer moved: during a drag the meter follows it; otherwise the item under it is
        the hot one (the keyboard's cursor stays where it is) — only the two that changed are
        redrawn."""
        self.pointer = (x, y)
        if dragging and self.dragging:
            m = self.item(self.dragging)
            v = self.meter_at(m, x, held=True) if m else None
            if v is not None:
                self.set_level(m.key, v)
            return
        self.rehot()

    def enter(self, x, y):
        """The pointer came over the panel (or the panel opened under it): what is there is hot
        at once, before any motion."""
        self.pointer = (x, y)
        if not self.dragging:
            self.rehot()

    def leave(self):
        """The pointer left the panel: nothing is under it."""
        self.pointer = None
        self.set_hot(None)

    def rehot(self):
        """The hot item: the one under the pointer now (the layout may have moved under it).
        In a menu the cursor goes with it: one cursor, the pointer's or the keys' — whichever
        moved last (it stays where the pointer left it)."""
        it = self.at(*self.pointer) if self.pointer else None
        if it and self.mode == "menu" and self.visible and not self.dragging:
            self.move(it.key)
        self.set_hot(it.key if it else None)

    def set_hot(self, key):
        if key != self.hot:
            old, self.hot = self.item(self.hot) if self.hot else None, key
            changed = [i for i in (old, self.item(key) if key else None) if i]
            if changed:                                # (none: nothing to redraw — not all)
                self.ui.redraw(*changed)

    def press(self, x, y):
        """A click does what is under it, there and then (the pointer's item is hot whether
        or not a motion said so). The keyboard's cursor, if a key has shown it, moves there;
        none is shown for a click."""
        self.pointer = (x, y)
        it = self.at(x, y)
        self.set_hot(it.key if it else None)
        if not it:
            return
        if self.focus is not None:
            self.move(it.key)
        if it.kind == "meter":
            v = self.meter_at(it, x)
            if v is not None:
                self.dragging = it.key
                self.set_level(it.key, v)
                return
        self.activate(it.key)

    def release(self, x=None, y=None):
        key, self.dragging = self.dragging, None
        if x is not None and self.visible:
            self.pointer = (x, y)
            self.rehot()                               # (a drag may end anywhere)
        if key and key not in self.busy and key not in self.wanted:
            self.refresh()                             # (its level confirmed: read back)

    def wheel(self, x, y, up):
        if self.view:
            self.scroll_list((-3 if up else 3) * (METER_H + METER_GAP))
            return
        it = self.at(x, y)
        if it and (it.kind == "meter" or it.key in WHEELED):
            self.nudge(it.key, up)

    def nudge(self, key, up):
        """The wheel's notch on a meter (5 either way) or on a tile it changes (kawa-quick)."""
        if key in WHEELED:
            self.ui.run(["kawa-quick", "--do", WHEELED[key][0 if up else 1]], lambda rc, out: self.refresh())
        else:
            self.step(key, 5 if up else -5)

    def scroll_list(self, dy):
        """A menu's list moved by dy px (the wheel: three rows a notch), within its ends."""
        top, h, full = self.view
        new = max(0, min(full - h, int(self.scroll + dy)))
        if new != self.scroll:
            self.scroll = new
            if self.pointer:
                self.rehot()
            self.ui.redraw()

    def scroll_by(self, x, y, dy):
        """A smooth scroll (dy < 0: up): a wheel's notch is 1, a touchpad sends small parts —
        summed, a step per whole one, so a touchpad does not race through the levels."""
        if self.view:
            self.scroll_list(dy * 3 * (METER_H + METER_GAP))
            return
        it = self.at(x, y)
        if not it or (it.kind != "meter" and it.key not in WHEELED):
            self.scrolled = (None, 0.0)
            return
        key, acc = self.scrolled
        acc = (acc if key == it.key else 0.0) - dy
        while abs(acc) >= 1 - 1e-9:
            self.nudge(it.key, acc > 0)
            acc -= 1 if acc > 0 else -1
        self.scrolled = (it.key, acc)


def mark(what, on):
    """Its button on the bar told the panel (what: a mode) or the dropdown (what: "menu
    CALLER") is open — its marker holds this pid (and a dropdown's, its caller) — or closed
    (no marker)."""
    path, text = (MARKS[what], f"{os.getpid()}\n") if what in MARKS else \
        (DROP, f"{os.getpid()} {what.partition(' ')[2]}\n")
    try:
        if on:
            with open(path + ".new", "w") as f:
                f.write(text)
            os.replace(path + ".new", path)
        else:
            os.unlink(path)
    except OSError:
        pass


def bar_push(name, line):
    """A value pushed to the bar's long-running module name (volume, brightness), as kawa-bar's
    bar_push does: line into each $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kawa-bar.NAME.PID, opened read-write and
    without waiting — drawn by the module at once."""
    try:
        names = [f for f in os.listdir(RUN) if f.startswith(f"kawa-bar.{name}.")]
    except OSError:
        return
    for f in names:
        try:
            os.kill(int(f.rsplit(".", 1)[1]), 0)
            fd = os.open(os.path.join(RUN, f), os.O_RDWR | os.O_NONBLOCK)
            try:
                os.write(fd, (line + "\n").encode())
            finally:
                os.close(fd)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass


def poke_sigs(what):
    """The bar's signals (SIGRTMIN+N) that tell what's button (a mode, or "menu CALLER") to look
    again — quick settings' with the mode seal's (its click opens them too) —, [] for the
    timer's (a long-running module: woken), None for no button."""
    if what in POKE:
        return [POKE[what]] + ([12] if what == "quick" else [])
    caller = what.partition(" ")[2]
    if caller not in DROP_POKE:
        return None
    return [int(DROP_POKE[caller])] if DROP_POKE[caller] else []


_bar = [0]


def waybar():
    """The bar's process (our waybar, the newest), remembered while it lives; 0: none."""
    p = _bar[0]
    try:
        if p and open(f"/proc/{p}/comm").read().strip() == "waybar":
            return p
    except OSError:
        pass
    found = []
    for d in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if d.isdigit():
            try:
                if open(f"/proc/{d}/comm").read().strip() == "waybar" and os.stat(f"/proc/{d}").st_uid == os.getuid():
                    found.append((os.stat(f"/proc/{d}").st_mtime, int(d)))
            except OSError:
                pass
    _bar[0] = max(found)[1] if found else 0
    return _bar[0]


def poke(what):
    """what's button told at once, as kawa-dwlstatus --poke tells it, but from here (no process
    started on the way): SIGRTMIN+N to waybar once it catches it (until it does, the signal
    would end it), and the bar's long-running modules (time, memory, volume) woken through
    their fifos ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kawa-bar.wake.PID, opened read-write: never waits)."""
    sigs = poke_sigs(what)
    if sigs is None:
        return
    pid = waybar() if sigs else 0
    if pid:
        try:
            caught = next(int(l.split()[1], 16) for l in open(f"/proc/{pid}/status") if l.startswith("SigCgt:"))
            for n in sigs:
                if caught >> (signal.SIGRTMIN + n - 1) & 1:
                    os.kill(pid, signal.SIGRTMIN + n)
        except (OSError, StopIteration, ValueError):
            pass
    try:
        wakes = [f for f in os.listdir(RUN) if f.startswith("kawa-bar.wake.")]
    except OSError:
        return
    for f in wakes:
        path = os.path.join(RUN, f)
        try:
            os.kill(int(f.rsplit(".", 1)[1]), 0)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            try:
                os.unlink(path)                       # (its module gone)
            except OSError:
                pass
            continue
        try:
            fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_NONBLOCK)
            try:
                os.write(fd, b"\n")
            finally:
                os.close(fd)
        except OSError:
            pass


def stale_marks():
    """The markers a panel that ended without closing (killed, crashed) left: taken away, as
    it starts; → the modes whose module must be told (poked)."""
    gone = []
    for mode in MODES + ("launcher",):
        if os.path.lexists(MARKS[mode]):
            try:
                os.unlink(MARKS[mode])
            except OSError:
                pass
            gone.append(mode)
    return gone


def running():
    """The panel running now (its pid file, that pid's command line), or None."""
    try:
        pid = int(open(PIDFILE).read())
        if pid != os.getpid() and b"kawa-panel" in open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb").read():
            return pid
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    return None


def ask(line):
    """The running panel asked (a request line into its fifo: opened read-write, so it never
    waits; one write, so it arrives whole)."""
    fd = os.open(FIFO, os.O_RDWR | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    try:
        os.write(fd, (line + "\n").encode())
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def menu_args(args):
    """A menu's options (kawa-menu's, as it passes them) → the request's, or None (wrong use):
    --drop, --index, --stay, --password, --title TEXT, --width N, --lines N, --select N, --prompt TEXT."""
    req = {"drop": False, "index": False, "stay": False, "password": False, "title": "", "width": 0, "lines": 0,
           "select": None, "prompt": None}
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a in ("--drop", "--index", "--stay", "--password"):
            req[a[2:]] = True
            i += 1
            continue
        if a not in ("--title", "--width", "--lines", "--select", "--prompt") or i + 1 >= len(args):
            return None
        v = args[i + 1]
        if a in ("--width", "--lines", "--select"):
            if not v.isdigit():
                return None
            v = int(v)
        req[a[2:]] = v
        i += 2
    return req


def menu_request(text):
    """kawa-menu's request file → (its caller, Menu), or None (not one): the caller, the number
    of options, the options, then the rows — a line each."""
    lines = text.split("\n")
    if lines and lines[-1] == "":
        lines.pop()                               # (the last line's own end)
    if len(lines) < 2 or not lines[1].isdigit() or len(lines) < 2 + int(lines[1]):
        return None
    n = int(lines[1])
    req = menu_args(lines[2:2 + n])
    if req is None:
        return None
    return lines[0], Menu(lines[2 + n:], drop=req["drop"], title=req["title"], width=req["width"],
                          lines_shown=req["lines"], select=req["select"], prompt=req["prompt"],
                          index=req["index"], stay=req["stay"], password=req["password"])


# ── on screen ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def main(argv):
    if len(argv) > 1 and argv[1] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0
    flags = {(): None, ("--open",): "quick", ("--sound",): "sound", ("--brightness",): "brightness"}
    if tuple(argv[1:]) not in flags:
        print("usage: kawa-panel [--open | --sound | --brightness] (kawa-panel --help)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    want = flags[tuple(argv[1:])]
    # one at a time: another one running is asked instead
    if running():
        if want:
            ask("toggle " + want)
        return 0
    # the fifo first, before anything slow (GTK): a request that comes while the panel is still
    # being built (right after login, or after a restart) waits in it, kept
    try:
        os.unlink(FIFO + ".new")
    except OSError:
        pass
    os.mkfifo(FIFO + ".new", 0o600)
    fifo = os.open(FIFO + ".new", os.O_RDWR | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    os.replace(FIFO + ".new", FIFO)
    with open(PIDFILE, "w") as f:
        f.write(str(os.getpid()))
    import gi
    gi.require_version("Gtk", "3.0")
    gi.require_version("Gdk", "3.0")
    gi.require_version("GtkLayerShell", "0.1")
    gi.require_version("PangoCairo", "1.0")
    gi.require_version("GLibUnix", "2.0")    # (the signals: GLib's own way is deprecated)
    gi.require_version("GioUnix", "2.0")     # (the launcher's entries: Gio's DesktopAppInfo is deprecated)
    from gi.repository import Gdk, Gio, GioUnix, GLib, GLibUnix, Gtk, GtkLayerShell, Pango, PangoCairo
    import cairo

    # a marker left by a panel that ended without closing (killed, crashed): its module dark
    for mode in stale_marks():
        poke(mode)
    # no transitions anywhere in it (a tooltip's fade, a theme's hover easing): every change of
    # state is drawn at once — the drawing is the panel's own, the rest must not animate. No
    # GTK widget can show a hover background either: the window holds one drawing area, as big
    # as it, and draw() paints every pixel of it (the surface first), so whatever a theme gives
    # window:hover or drawingarea:hover is underneath; the one other widget, the wallpaper's
    # tooltip, opens beside the pointer, not under it (no buttons or other widgets: tested)
    css = Gtk.CssProvider()
    css.load_from_data(b"* { transition: none; animation: none; }")
    Gtk.StyleContext.add_provider_for_screen(Gdk.Screen.get_default(), css,
                                             Gtk.STYLE_PROVIDER_PRIORITY_USER + 1)
    ic = icons()
    pal = {}
    see = [glass()]                  # the ground's alpha, as the terminal's (read at each opening)

    def restyle():
        pal.clear()
        pal.update(colours(os.path.join(CURRENT, "panel.conf")))

    restyle()

    # a window of its own each time it opens (each menu its own): one
    # hidden and shown again can come back without the keyboard and the mouse, after a
    # click outside took them from it
    # — made ahead, though: the next one waits built and realized (spare), so an open only
    # places it and maps it; the first after login costs no more than any other
    view = {"win": None, "area": None, "spare": None}

    def build_window():
        """A panel window, all but its place, title and size: its surface, its keys, its events."""
        win = Gtk.Window()
        # a see-through ground, as the terminal's (a panel and a menu are text screens too):
        # an RGBA surface, painted whole by the drawing — the wallpaper shows a little through
        rgba = win.get_screen().get_rgba_visual()
        if rgba is not None:
            win.set_visual(rgba)
        win.set_app_paintable(True)
        see[0] = glass()
        GtkLayerShell.init_for_window(win)
        GtkLayerShell.set_namespace(win, "kawa-panel")
        GtkLayerShell.set_layer(win, GtkLayerShell.Layer.OVERLAY)
        # the keyboard its own while open (exclusive): every key reaches it at once,
        # never a window behind — and dwl treats it as a menu: a click outside or a workspace
        # switch takes the keyboard back, which closes it (focus-out). On-demand would leave
        # it open behind a click, and its keys to whoever has the focus
        GtkLayerShell.set_keyboard_mode(win, GtkLayerShell.KeyboardMode.EXCLUSIVE)
        # the keys and the mouse are the window's own (the drawing fills it, pixel for pixel);
        # the pointer's coming in counts as much as its moving: enter-notify lights what is
        # under it at once (it opened under the pointer, or the pointer came back and stopped)
        win.add_events(Gdk.EventMask.POINTER_MOTION_MASK | Gdk.EventMask.BUTTON_PRESS_MASK
                       | Gdk.EventMask.BUTTON_RELEASE_MASK | Gdk.EventMask.SCROLL_MASK
                       | Gdk.EventMask.SMOOTH_SCROLL_MASK | Gdk.EventMask.KEY_PRESS_MASK
                       | Gdk.EventMask.ENTER_NOTIFY_MASK | Gdk.EventMask.LEAVE_NOTIFY_MASK)
        win.set_has_tooltip(True)
        area = Gtk.DrawingArea()
        win.add(area)
        area.connect("draw", draw)
        win.connect("key-press-event", on_key)
        win.connect("motion-notify-event", on_motion)
        win.connect("enter-notify-event", on_enter)
        win.connect("leave-notify-event", on_leave)
        win.connect("button-press-event", on_press)
        win.connect("button-release-event", on_release)
        win.connect("scroll-event", on_scroll)
        win.connect("query-tooltip", on_tip)
        # a click outside (dwl takes the keyboard back), or another menu taking it: closed.
        # Only this window's: the one before (just replaced — another mode, or opened again
        # at once) loses the keyboard too, a moment later, and must not close this one
        win.connect("focus-out-event", lambda w, ev: (w is view["win"] and panel.close("lost")) or False)
        # taken off the screen some other way (the compositor closed its surface): closed too,
        # so the bar's cat never stays lit over nothing
        win.connect("unmap-event", lambda w, ev: (w is view["win"] and panel.close("lost")) or False)
        area.show()
        win.realize()
        return win, area

    def spare():
        if view["spare"] is None:
            view["spare"] = build_window()
        return False

    def make_window():
        win, area = view["spare"] or build_window()
        view["spare"] = None
        GLib.timeout_add(200, spare)            # (the next one, once this one is drawn)
        win.set_title(TITLE[panel.mode])
        # under the bar, 8 px from it: under the click on its output (the output whose x it is),
        # or, opened by its key, quick settings 8 px from the screen's edge (as kawa-menu --drop:
        # its border in line with a window's there), the others under their module
        # (a menu not dropped from the bar — power, help, change —: no anchor, the middle)
        side, margin, out_x = panel.place
        if side != "centre":
            edge = GtkLayerShell.Edge.LEFT if side == "left" else GtkLayerShell.Edge.RIGHT
            GtkLayerShell.set_anchor(win, GtkLayerShell.Edge.TOP, True)
            GtkLayerShell.set_anchor(win, edge, True)
            GtkLayerShell.set_margin(win, GtkLayerShell.Edge.TOP, 8)
            GtkLayerShell.set_margin(win, edge, margin)
        if side == "left":
            disp = Gdk.Display.get_default()
            for i in range(disp.get_n_monitors()):
                if disp.get_monitor(i).get_geometry().x == out_x:
                    GtkLayerShell.set_monitor(win, disp.get_monitor(i))
        area.set_size_request(*panel.win)
        view["win"], view["area"], view["size"] = win, area, panel.win
        return win

    drawing = []            # (the drawing ready: the pads can be measured)

    class UI:
        def trim(self, size):
            """(dy, h, w): the content's ink, first and last row (drawn off screen, at rest), PAD
            px of paper from the frame above and below it — the window's top dy px down the
            layout, h high; and its last column PAD px from the frame on the right — w wide (a
            stitch stops on its last whole stitch, up to 4 px short of the layout's edge: the
            panel ends PAD px after it, not PAD after the layout's edge)."""
            W, H = size
            if not drawing:
                return 0, H, W                    # (not drawable yet: the layout's own)
            surf = cairo.ImageSurface(cairo.FORMAT_ARGB32, W, H)
            content(cairo.Context(surf), at_rest=True)
            surf.flush()
            data, stride = surf.get_data(), surf.get_stride()
            blank = bytes(stride)
            rows = [y for y in range(BORDER, H - BORDER) if data[y * stride:(y + 1) * stride] != blank]
            if not rows:
                return 0, H, W
            dy = rows[0] - (BORDER + PAD)
            right = max(len(bytes(data[y * stride:(y + 1) * stride]).rstrip(b"\0")) for y in rows)   # (bytes: 4 a pixel)
            return dy, rows[-1] + 1 - dy + PAD + BORDER, min(W, (right + 3) // 4 + PAD + BORDER)

        def redraw(self, *items):
            """The items given (their own pixels: no flicker, little to draw), or all."""
            a = view["area"]
            if not a:
                return
            if items and view.get("size") == panel.win and not panel.view:
                for it in items:
                    a.queue_draw_area(it.x, it.y - panel.dy, it.w, it.h)
                return
            if view.get("size") != panel.win:
                view["size"] = panel.win
                a.set_size_request(*panel.win)
                if view["win"]:
                    view["win"].resize(*panel.win)    # (a page in place may be narrower: shrinks too)
            a.queue_draw()

        def show(self):
            self.hide()
            make_window().show_all()

        def later(self, ms, fn):
            GLib.timeout_add(ms, lambda: fn() and False)

        def hide(self):
            if view["win"]:
                w, view["win"], view["area"] = view["win"], None, None
                w.hide()
                GLib.idle_add(w.destroy)            # (not inside its own focus-out handler)

        def light(self, what, on):
            mark(what, on)
            poke(what)

        def push(self, name, line):
            bar_push(name, line)

        def click(self):
            """The last press on the bar, while fresh: its id (dwl writes the file anew each
            time — a new inode), or None."""
            try:
                st = os.stat(POINTER)
            except OSError:
                return None
            return f"{st.st_ino}.{st.st_mtime_ns}" if -FRESH < time.time() - st.st_mtime < FRESH else None

        def clicked(self):
            """The bar's click that opened it (dwl's record, fresh), or None (its key)."""
            try:
                with open(POINTER) as f:
                    text = f.read()
                age = time.time() - os.stat(POINTER).st_mtime
            except OSError:
                return None
            return bar_click(text, age)

        # the launcher's doing: an entry started, an answer copied, a command run
        def start(self, app):
            info = app.info
            try:
                argv = shlex.split(re.sub(r"%[fFuUick]", "", info.get_commandline() or "").replace("%%", "%"))
            except ValueError:
                argv = []
            if not argv:
                return
            if info.get_boolean("Terminal"):
                argv = ["footclient", "--"] + argv
            # (setsid -f: its own session, no child of the panel's — a panel restarted keeps it)
            self.launch(["setsid", "-f", "--"] + argv)
            apps.started(app.id)

        def copy_answer(self, text):
            self.launch(["bash", "-c", '. "$HOME/.config/fedokawaii/files.sh"; . "$HOME/.config/fedokawaii/icons.sh"\n'
                         'if printf %s "$1" | clip_put "text/plain;charset=utf-8"; then\n'
                         '  notify-send -a desktop -- "$ICON_CLIPBOARD  copied" "$1"\n'
                         'else notify-send -a desktop -- "$ICON_CLIPBOARD  not copied" "$1 · no clipboard"; fi', "_", text])

        def command(self, text):
            try:
                argv = shlex.split(text)
            except ValueError:
                argv = []
            if argv and shutil.which(argv[0]):
                self.launch(["setsid", "-f", "--"] + argv)
            else:
                self.launch(["bash", "-c", '. "$HOME/.config/fedokawaii/icons.sh"; '
                             'notify-send -a desktop -- "$ICON_CHEVRON  launcher" "nothing found for: $1"', "_", text])

        def calc(self, m):
            calc_ask(m)

        def run(self, argv, done):
            p = Gio.Subprocess.new(argv, Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDOUT_PIPE | Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDERR_SILENCE)

            def fin(proc, res):
                try:
                    _, out, _ = proc.communicate_utf8_finish(res)
                except GLib.Error:
                    out = ""
                done(proc.get_exit_status() if proc.get_if_exited() else 1, out or "")
            p.communicate_utf8_async(None, None, fin)

        def launch(self, argv):
            Gio.Subprocess.new(argv, Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDOUT_SILENCE | Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDERR_SILENCE)

        def listen(self, on):
            """The clean mic's level: on — pw-record from it (16 kHz mono, raw samples on its
            stdout, 50 ms a block), each block's peak to panel.heard; off — that recorder
            ended. (The chain runs only while something records from it: this, while shown.)"""
            p = rec["p"]
            if on and p is None:
                p = rec["p"] = Gio.Subprocess.new(
                    ["pw-record", "--raw", "--target", "clean_mic", "--rate", "16000", "--channels", "1",
                     "--format", "s16", "--latency", "50ms", "-"],
                    Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDOUT_PIPE | Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDERR_SILENCE)

                def got(stream, res):
                    try:
                        b = stream.read_bytes_finish(res).get_data()
                    except GLib.Error:
                        b = b""
                    if rec["p"] is not p:             # (ended meanwhile)
                        return
                    if not b:
                        rec["p"] = None               # (pw-record ended by itself: the next read starts one)
                        return
                    panel.heard(peak_pct(b))
                    stream.read_bytes_async(1600, GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, None, got)
                p.get_stdout_pipe().read_bytes_async(1600, GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, None, got)
            elif not on and p is not None:
                rec["p"] = None
                p.force_exit()

    rec = {"p": None}                                 # (the clean mic's level: its recorder)

    # ── the launcher's entries: Gio's list of the desktop entries shown (NoDisplay, Hidden,
    # OnlyShowIn already applied; ~/.local/share/applications first), each with its icon,
    # loaded once — read again when an entry is added, changed or removed (Gio's own watch)
    class Apps:
        def __init__(self):
            self.cache = None
            self.mon = Gio.AppInfoMonitor.get()
            self.mon.connect("changed", lambda *_: self.drop())

        def drop(self):
            self.cache = None

        def list(self):
            if self.cache is None:
                theme, got = Gtk.IconTheme.get_default(), []
                for info in Gio.AppInfo.get_all():
                    if not isinstance(info, GioUnix.DesktopAppInfo) or not info.should_show():
                        continue
                    a = App(info.get_id() or "", info.get_name() or "", info.get_generic_name(),
                            info.get_keywords(), info.get_string("X-Fedokawaii-Kanji"), info)
                    a.pic = a.kanji or self.icon(theme, info) or "窓"   # (none at all: a window's seal)
                    got.append(a)
                self.cache = got
            return self.cache

        @staticmethod
        def icon(theme, info):
            g = info.get_icon()
            if g is None:
                return None
            try:
                found = theme.lookup_by_gicon(g, APP_ICON, Gtk.IconLookupFlags.FORCE_SIZE)
                return found.load_icon() if found else None
            except GLib.Error:
                return None

        @staticmethod
        def counts():
            """How often each entry was started (id|count a line) — fuzzel's own record the first
            time, so the order you were used to stays."""
            out = {}
            for path in (COUNTS, os.path.join(HOME, ".cache/fuzzel")):
                try:
                    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                        for line in f:
                            k, _, n = line.rstrip("\n").rpartition("|")
                            if k and n.isdigit():
                                out[k] = int(n)
                    return out
                except OSError:
                    continue
            return out

        def started(self, aid):
            c = self.counts()
            c[aid] = c.get(aid, 0) + 1
            try:
                os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(COUNTS), exist_ok=True)
                with open(COUNTS + ".new", "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.writelines(f"{k}|{n}\n" for k, n in sorted(c.items()))
                os.replace(COUNTS + ".new", COUNTS)
            except OSError:
                pass

    apps = Apps()

    # ── the launcher's sums: one qalc for the panel, asked line by line (as kawa-calc asks
    # its own), started at the first sum typed and ended two minutes after the last ──
    SENT = "7777777.25"                               # (the line after each sum: its answer ends at it)
    NOT_SUMS = re.compile(r"(quit|exit|set|save|store|assume|base|mode|help|list|find|info|delete|keep|unkeep|"
                          r"function|variable|rpn|stack|clear|history|exrates|swap|rotate|copy|move|pop|to|"
                          r"convert|factor|expand|partial|simplify|approximate|exact)$")
    FORMS = re.compile(r"(hex|hexadecimal|bin|binary|oct|octal|dec|decimal|duo|duodecimal|roman|base.*|bases|"
                       r"fraction|frac|sci|scientific|eng|engineering|utc|time|sexa.*|factors|partial.*|cis|"
                       r"polar|rect.*|exact|approximate|optimal|prefix|bijective|unicode|calendars|fp.*|float|"
                       r"double|ieee.*|si|cgs|imperial|us)$")
    qc = {"p": None, "inp": None, "out": None, "busy": None, "next": None, "idle": 0}

    def sum_of(text):
        """What qalc is asked for what is typed, or None: no digit (a word alone is no sum —
        "steam" is not one), one of qalc's own commands; "15% of 2400" is qalc's "15% * 2400"."""
        e = text.strip()
        if not re.search(r"[0-9]", e) or NOT_SUMS.match(e.split()[0].lower()):
            return None
        while True:
            m = re.match(r"^(.*[0-9.])\s*%\s*[Oo][Ff]\s+(.*)$", e)
            if not m:
                break
            e = f"{m.group(1)}% * {m.group(2)}"
        # (a conversion: "in" is "to", into the unit asked alone — kawa-calc's rule, the same)
        m = re.match(r"^(.*\S)\s+(?:[Tt][Oo]|[Ii][Nn])\s+([^\W\d_$€£¥°][^\s]*|[$€£¥°]\S*)$", e)
        if m:
            t = m.group(2)
            if not FORMS.match(t.lower()):
                t = "-" + t
            e = f"{m.group(1)} to {t}"
        return e

    def money(a):
        """An amount of money to the cent (one under one as qalc has it), as kawa-calc does."""
        m = re.match(r"^([A-Z]{3} |[$€£¥])(-?[0-9]+\.[0-9]+)$", a)
        if m and not m.group(2).lstrip("-").startswith("0."):
            return f"{m.group(1)}{float(m.group(2)):.2f}"
        return a

    def calc_ask(m):
        q = m.query
        if sum_of(q) is None:
            return
        qc["next"] = (m, q)
        if qc["busy"] is None:
            calc_send()

    def calc_start():
        try:
            p = Gio.Subprocess.new(["qalc", "-s", "upxrates 7", "-t", "-s", "color 0", "-s", "unicode 0"],
                                   Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDIN_PIPE | Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDOUT_PIPE
                                   | Gio.SubprocessFlags.STDERR_MERGE)
        except GLib.Error:
            return False
        qc["p"], qc["inp"] = p, p.get_stdin_pipe()
        qc["out"] = Gio.DataInputStream.new(p.get_stdout_pipe())
        p.wait_async(None, lambda proc, res, p=p: qc.update(p=None) if qc["p"] is p else None)   # (ended: forgotten)
        return True

    def calc_send():
        m, q = qc["next"]
        qc["next"] = None
        if qc["p"] is None and not calc_start():
            return
        qc["busy"], qc["ans"] = (m, q), None
        try:
            qc["inp"].write_all(f"{sum_of(q)}\n{SENT}\n".encode(), None)
        except GLib.Error:
            qc["p"], qc["busy"] = None, None
            return
        qc["out"].read_line_async(GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, None, calc_line)

    def calc_line(stream, res):
        try:
            line, _ = stream.read_line_finish_utf8(res)
        except GLib.Error:
            line = None
        if line is None:                              # (qalc gone: a new one at the next sum)
            qc["p"], qc["busy"] = None, None
            return
        if line != "  " + SENT:
            if qc["ans"] is None and line.startswith("  ") and line.strip():
                qc["ans"] = line[2:]
            stream.read_line_async(GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, None, calc_line)
            return
        (m, q), a = qc["busy"], qc["ans"]
        qc["busy"] = None
        e = sum_of(q)
        if a and not a.startswith(("error", "warning")) and a != e:
            m.calc = (q, money(a))
            panel.answered(m)
        if qc["next"]:
            calc_send()
        qc["idle"] += 1
        n = qc["idle"]
        GLib.timeout_add_seconds(120, lambda: calc_end(n))

    def calc_end(n):
        if n == qc["idle"] and qc["busy"] is None and qc["p"] is not None:
            qc["p"].force_exit()
            qc["p"] = None
        return False

    panel = Panel(UI())

    # ── drawing: every square on whole pixels, the letters unsmoothed ──
    fo = cairo.FontOptions()
    fo.set_antialias(cairo.ANTIALIAS_NONE)
    fo.set_hint_style(cairo.HINT_STYLE_FULL)
    fo.set_hint_metrics(cairo.HINT_METRICS_ON)
    font = Pango.FontDescription.from_string("Sumi UI, Sumi Icons")
    font.set_absolute_size(LINE * Pango.SCALE)

    def col(cr, name):
        cr.set_source_rgb(*pal[name])

    def box(cr, x, y, w, h, name):
        col(cr, name)
        cr.rectangle(x, y, w, h)
        cr.fill()

    def frame(cr, x, y, w, h, name, t=1):
        box(cr, x, y, w, t, name)
        box(cr, x, y + h - t, w, t, name)
        box(cr, x, y + t, t, h - 2 * t, name)
        box(cr, x + w - t, y + t, t, h - 2 * t, name)

    def text_layout(cr, s):
        lay = PangoCairo.create_layout(cr)
        PangoCairo.context_set_font_options(lay.get_context(), fo)
        lay.set_font_description(font)
        lay.set_text(s, -1)
        return lay

    def text(cr, x, y, s, name, right=False, width=None, tail=False):
        """s on the line whose LINE px box starts at y, within width if given (the layouts make
        every text fit; anything wider is cut at its end, the typed text at its start — never
        with "…")."""
        if width is not None and px_of(s) > width:
            s = fit(s, width, tail=tail)
        lay = text_layout(cr, s)
        ink, log = lay.get_pixel_extents()
        col(cr, name)
        # (right-aligned: its ink ends at x — a value's last pixel on the content's edge)
        cr.move_to(x - (ink.x + ink.width) if right else x, y - log.y - (log.height - LINE))
        PangoCairo.show_layout(cr, lay)
        return (ink.x + ink.width) if right else log.width

    def glyph(cr, x, y, ch, name, box_w=None, box_h=None):
        """An icon or a kanji, its ink centred in a box_w × box_h box at x, y (a line if not given)."""
        lay = text_layout(cr, ch)
        ink, log = lay.get_pixel_extents()
        col(cr, name)
        # (in a box: its ink centred; on a line: its ink centred in the line's square, LINE
        # wide from x — every row's glyph on one axis, whatever its side bearing, and a wide
        # one (the volume's speaker and waves, 20 px) stands out 2 px either side of the
        # column instead of running into the name)
        gx = x + (((box_w or LINE) - ink.width) // 2 - ink.x)
        gy = y + ((box_h - ink.height) // 2 - ink.y if box_h else -log.y - (log.height - LINE))
        cr.move_to(gx, gy)
        PangoCairo.show_layout(cr, lay)

    def seal(cr, x, y, ch, stamped, on_fill):
        """A tile's seal. On: stamped in vermilion ink as a real hanko is — the kanji and a
        2 px frame (朱文: red letters on the paper, lighter than the day's filled seal at the
        top, so nine of them never shout). Off: a faint 1 px outline, the kanji faint."""
        if STAMP == "filled" and stamped:
            box(cr, x, y, SEAL, SEAL, "text_on_accent" if on_fill else "accent")
            glyph(cr, x, y, ch, "accent" if on_fill else "text_on_accent", SEAL, SEAL)
        elif stamped:
            frame(cr, x, y, SEAL, SEAL, "text_on_accent" if on_fill else "accent_text", 2)
            glyph(cr, x, y, ch, "text_on_accent" if on_fill else "accent_text", SEAL, SEAL)
        else:
            frame(cr, x, y, SEAL, SEAL, "accent_lo" if on_fill else "edge2")
            glyph(cr, x, y, ch, "accent_lo" if on_fill else "faint", SEAL, SEAL)

    def stitch(cr, x, y, w):
        """A running stitch, 3 px on and 2 off, from x (a whole stitch first: every heading
        starts alike, a gap after its name) to the last whole stitch that fits in w — it may
        stop up to 4 px short of x + w (the content's edge, or a gap before a value)."""
        col(cr, "edge2")
        for sx in range(x, x + w - 2, 5):
            cr.rectangle(sx, y, 3, 1)
        cr.fill()

    def heading(cr, kanji, name, x, y, value=""):
        """A part of a panel or a menu: its kanji in a seal (a 1 px frame, the kanji vermilion
        — a label, not a state: quieter than a tile's stamp), its name, and the stitch on to
        the right edge, through the seal's middle (to a value at its end, quiet)."""
        right = panel.size[0] - PAD - BORDER
        frame(cr, x, y, SEAL, SEAL, "edge2")
        glyph(cr, x, y, kanji, "accent_text", SEAL, SEAL)
        ty = y + (SEAL - LINE) // 2
        w = text(cr, x + SEAL + GAP, ty, name, "text")
        if value:
            right -= text(cr, right, ty, value, "muted", right=True) + GAP
        sx = x + SEAL + GAP + w + GAP
        stitch(cr, sx, y + SEAL // 2, right - sx)

    def mark_(cr, m):
        """What a menu draws but does not offer: its title, a row not to be chosen, a grid's
        cell, the filter's line, "none" found."""
        k = m[0]
        if k == "note":
            _, x, y, w, t = m
            text(cr, x + GAP, y + (METER_H - LINE) // 2, t, "muted", width=w - 2 * GAP)
        elif k in SHOWN_KINDS:            # (a row not to choose: off faint, info in a row's inks)
            _, x, y, w, g, name, detail, value = m
            off = k == "off"
            dx = detail_x[0]
            ty = y + (METER_H - LINE) // 2
            glyph(cr, x + GAP, ty, g, "faint" if off else "muted")
            vr = min(value_r[0], x + w - GAP) if value_r[0] else x + w - GAP
            right = vr - ((text(cr, vr, ty, value, "faint" if off else "muted", right=True) + 2 * GAP) if value else 0)
            text(cr, x + NAME_X, ty, name, "faint" if off else "text", width=(dx if detail else right) - (x + NAME_X) - GAP)
            if detail:
                text(cr, dx, ty, detail, "faint" if off else "text", width=right - dx)
        elif k == "cell":
            _, x, y, w, h, t, state = m
            if state == "today":                  # (stamped, as a lit seal)
                frame(cr, x, y - 1, w, h + 2, "accent_text", 2)
            lw = text_layout(cr, t).get_pixel_extents()[1].width
            name = {"today": "accent_text", "dim": "faint", "head": "muted"}.get(state, "text")
            text(cr, x + (w - lw) // 2, y + (h - LINE) // 2, t, name)
        elif k == "input":
            _, x, y, w, h, prompt, query = m
            box(cr, x, y, w, h, "key")
            frame(cr, x, y, w, h, "edge")
            ty = y + (h - LINE) // 2
            pw = text(cr, x + GAP, ty, prompt, "muted") if prompt else 0
            qw = text(cr, x + GAP + pw, ty, query, "text", width=w - 2 * GAP - pw - 2, tail=True)
            box(cr, x + GAP + pw + qw + 1, ty, 2, LINE, "accent_text")   # (the caret: still, no blink)
        elif k == "none":
            _, x, y = m
            text(cr, x + NAME_X, y + (METER_H - LINE) // 2, "none", "faint")

    detail_x = [0]
    value_r = [0]           # (a menu's values' right edge; 0: each row's own right edge)

    def draw(widget, cr):
        W, H = panel.win
        # the ground: the terminal's own, at its alpha (kawa-glass) — the same see-through
        # cloth as every text screen; everything on it drawn opaque, as the terminal's text
        cr.set_operator(cairo.OPERATOR_SOURCE)
        cr.set_source_rgba(*pal["ground"], see[0])
        cr.paint()
        cr.set_operator(cairo.OPERATOR_OVER)
        cr.save()
        cr.translate(0, -panel.dy)
        content(cr)
        cr.restore()
        frame(cr, 0, 0, W, H, "muted", BORDER)
        return True

    def content(cr, at_rest=False):
        """Everything inside the frame, in the layout's own place (at rest: no cursor, nothing
        under the pointer — as the pads are measured)."""
        if at_rest:
            keep = panel.focus, panel.hot
            panel.focus = panel.hot = None
            try:
                content(cr)
            finally:
                panel.focus, panel.hot = keep
            return
        W, H = panel.size
        hx, hy = panel.head
        for ry in panel.rules:
            stitch(cr, hx, ry, INNER)
        # (a menu's list that scrolls: what is in it drawn moved up, cut to its view)
        top, vh = (panel.view[0], panel.view[1]) if panel.view else (H, 0)

        def listed(y, draw_it):
            if y < top:
                draw_it()
                return
            cr.save()
            cr.rectangle(0, top, W, vh)
            cr.clip()
            cr.translate(0, -panel.scroll)
            draw_it()
            cr.restore()
        # a menu's details in a column of their own: after its longest name (of a row with one)
        # and, in a menu, its values right-aligned in a column two gaps after the widest words
        # (columns(), as the menu's width was worked out — measured here in the font itself)
        rows = [("", it.name, it.detail, it.value) for it in panel.items if it.kind == "row"]
        rows += [m[4:8] for m in panel.marks if m[0] in SHOWN_KINDS]
        dx, vr = columns(rows, lambda t: text_layout(cr, t).get_pixel_extents()[1].width)
        detail_x[0] = hx + dx
        value_r[0] = hx + vr if panel.mode == "menu" and vr else 0
        for hd in panel.heads:
            listed(hd[3], lambda hd=hd: heading(cr, *hd))
        for x, y in panel.nones:
            text(cr, x + NAME_X, y + (METER_H - LINE) // 2, "none", "faint")
        for m in panel.marks:
            listed(m[2], lambda m=m: mark_(cr, m))
        for it in panel.items:
            listed(it.y if it.scrolls else 0, lambda it=it: item_(cr, it))
        return True

    def item_(cr, it):
        """An item: its fill and frame, its glyph, name, value (look() says in which colours)."""
        # the keyboard's cursor filled in vermilion; the pointer's item only its glyph and
        # name lit (look(), above main) — no box behind it, no easing: drawn at once
        lk = look(it, it.key == panel.focus, it.key == panel.hot)
        on = lk["on_fill"]
        if lk["fill"]:
            box(cr, it.x, it.y, it.w, it.h, lk["fill"])
        if lk["frame"]:
            frame(cr, it.x, it.y, it.w, it.h, lk["frame"])
        if it.kind in ("meter", "level"):
            ty = it.y + (it.h - LINE) // 2
            pct = percent(it.value)
            mark = it.kanji or ic[it.icon or vol_icon(pct, it.muted)]
            inset = getattr(it, "inset", GAP)
            glyph(cr, it.x + inset, ty, mark, lk["glyph"])
            text(cr, it.x + inset + LINE + GAP, ty, it.name, lk["label"])
            # (no DDC/CI, muted, no output: the words alone; a level's number is the bar's,
            # above — the slider stands where the sound's does)
            if pct is None:
                text(cr, it.x + it.w - GAP, ty, it.value, lk["value"], right=True)
                return
            sx = Panel.slider_x(it)
            mid = it.y + it.h // 2                      # the rule's middle: the row's
            # (on the vermilion fill everything in the colour of what stands on vermilion;
            # muted, the level kept but not heard: the fill hollow, the knob muted)
            fill, rest = ("text_on_accent", "accent_lo") if on else ("asagi", "faint")
            knob_ink = ("accent_lo" if on else "muted") if it.muted else ("text_on_accent" if on else "strong")
            w, k = fill_of(pct), knob_of(pct)
            live = it.kind == "level"                   # (a meter that only shows: no knob)
            end = w if live else k - 1                  # the fill up to the knob's air
            if end > 0:
                fy = mid - FILL_H // 2
                frame(cr, sx, fy, end, FILL_H, fill) if it.muted else box(cr, sx, fy, end, FILL_H, fill)
            t0 = w if live else k + KNOB + 1            # the track after the knob's air
            if t0 < SPAN:
                box(cr, sx + t0, mid - TRACK_H // 2, SPAN - t0, TRACK_H, rest)
                for tx in ticks():
                    if tx >= t0:
                        box(cr, sx + tx, mid - FILL_H // 2, 1, FILL_H, rest)
            if not live:
                box(cr, sx + k, mid - KNOB_H // 2, KNOB, KNOB_H, knob_ink)
        elif it.kind == "tile":
            glyph(cr, it.x + GAP, it.y + GAP, ic[it.icon], lk["glyph"], None, SEAL)
            seal(cr, it.x + it.w - GAP - SEAL, it.y + GAP, it.kanji, it.lit, on)
            tw = it.w - 2 * GAP
            text(cr, it.x + GAP, it.y + GAP + SEAL + 4, it.name, lk["label"], width=tw)
            text(cr, it.x + GAP, it.y + GAP + SEAL + 4 + LINE + 2, it.value, lk["value"], width=tw)
        elif it.kind == "app":                   # (the launcher's: its icon, its name, the answer's "copy")
            ty = it.y + (it.h - LINE) // 2
            ix, iy = it.x + GAP, it.y + (it.h - APP_ICON) // 2
            pic = "算" if it.app is None else it.app.pic
            if isinstance(pic, str):              # a seal: the kanji in a 1 px frame, on a tile's ground
                box(cr, ix, iy, APP_ICON, APP_ICON, "key")
                frame(cr, ix, iy, APP_ICON, APP_ICON, "edge2")
                glyph(cr, ix, iy, pic, "accent_text", APP_ICON, APP_ICON)
            else:
                Gdk.cairo_set_source_pixbuf(cr, pic, ix, iy)
                cr.paint()
            nx = ix + APP_ICON + GAP
            right = it.x + it.w - GAP
            if it.value:
                right -= text(cr, right, ty, it.value, lk["value"], right=True) + 2 * GAP
            text(cr, nx, ty, it.name, "accent_text" if it.app is None and not lk["fill"] else lk["label"],
                 width=right - nx)
        elif it.kind == "row":
            ty = it.y + (it.h - LINE) // 2
            g = it.glyph if it.glyph is not None else ic[it.icon]
            if g:
                glyph(cr, it.x + GAP, ty, g, lk["glyph"])
            right = min(value_r[0], it.x + it.w - GAP) if value_r[0] else it.x + it.w - GAP
            if it.value:
                right -= text(cr, right, ty, it.value, lk["value"], right=True) + 2 * GAP
            end = detail_x[0] - GAP if it.detail else right
            text(cr, it.x + NAME_X, ty, it.name, lk["label"], width=end - (it.x + NAME_X))
            if it.detail:
                text(cr, detail_x[0], ty, it.detail, lk["detail"], width=right - detail_x[0])
        else:
            ty = it.y + (it.h - LINE) // 2
            g = it.glyph if it.glyph is not None else ic[it.icon]
            if it.name:
                # (centred by ink: the glyph's and the name's own pixels, a gap apart)
                nink = text_layout(cr, it.name).get_pixel_extents()[0]
                gw = text_layout(cr, g).get_pixel_extents()[0].width if g else 0
                x = it.x + (it.w - (gw + GAP if g else 0) - nink.width) // 2
                if g:
                    glyph(cr, x, ty, g, lk["glyph"], gw)      # (its ink from x: a box its width)
                    x += gw + GAP
                text(cr, x - nink.x, ty, it.name, lk["label"])
            else:
                glyph(cr, it.x, it.y, g, lk["glyph"], it.w, it.h)

    drawing.append(True)                          # (from here on, every layout measured)
    panel.relayout()

    def on_key(w, ev):
        c = Gdk.keyval_to_unicode(ev.keyval)
        ctrl = ev.state & (Gdk.ModifierType.CONTROL_MASK | Gdk.ModifierType.MOD1_MASK | Gdk.ModifierType.MOD4_MASK)
        panel.key(Gdk.keyval_name(ev.keyval) or "", chr(c) if c and not ctrl else "")
        return True

    def on_motion(w, ev):
        panel.hover(ev.x, ev.y + panel.dy, dragging=bool(ev.state & Gdk.ModifierType.BUTTON1_MASK))
        return True

    def on_enter(w, ev):
        panel.enter(ev.x, ev.y + panel.dy)
        return False

    def on_leave(w, ev):
        # (the pointer gone into a child's window, or a grab's crossing, is still over the
        # panel: only a real leave)
        if real_leave(ev.mode.value_nick, ev.detail.value_nick):
            panel.leave()
        return False

    def on_press(w, ev):
        if ev.button == 1 and ev.type == Gdk.EventType.BUTTON_PRESS:
            panel.press(ev.x, ev.y + panel.dy)
        return True

    def on_release(w, ev):
        panel.release(ev.x, ev.y + panel.dy)
        return True

    def on_scroll(w, ev):
        if ev.direction == Gdk.ScrollDirection.SMOOTH:
            _, dx, dy = ev.get_scroll_deltas()
            if dy:
                panel.scroll_by(ev.x, ev.y + panel.dy, dy)
        elif ev.direction in (Gdk.ScrollDirection.UP, Gdk.ScrollDirection.DOWN):
            panel.wheel(ev.x, ev.y + panel.dy, ev.direction == Gdk.ScrollDirection.UP)
        return True

    def on_tip(w, x, y, kb, tip):
        it = panel.at(x, y + panel.dy)
        if it and it.tip:
            tip.set_text(it.tip)
            return True
        return False

    # a theme switch re-points ~/.config/fedokawaii/current: the new colours at once
    def on_theme():
        try:
            restyle()
        except OSError:
            return
        panel.ui.redraw()
    mon = watch_theme(Gio, on_theme)

    # the requests (the fifo, see the top): in the order they came, each answered at once
    def menu_from(pid):
        """kawa-menu PID's menu: its request read (and taken away), its answer fifo, and its
        end watched (pidfd: it ending first — killed — takes its menu away)."""
        req = os.path.join(RUN, f"kawa-menu.{pid}.req")
        out = os.path.join(RUN, f"kawa-menu.{pid}.out")
        try:
            with open(req) as f:
                got = menu_request(f.read())
            os.unlink(req)
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            got = None
        done = []

        def reply(rc, text):
            if done:
                return
            done.append(rc)
            try:
                fd = os.open(out, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
                try:
                    os.write(fd, f"{rc} {text}\n".encode())
                finally:
                    os.close(fd)
            except OSError:
                pass                                  # (kawa-menu gone: no one to tell)
        if got is None:
            reply(2, "")
            return
        try:
            pfd = os.pidfd_open(pid)
        except OSError:
            return                                    # (gone already: nothing to show)

        def ended(*_):
            os.close(pfd)
            done.append(-1)
            if panel.reply is reply:                  # (its menu still on screen: away with it)
                panel.reply = None
                panel.close("gone")
            return False
        GLib.io_add_watch(GLib.IOChannel.unix_new(pfd), GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, GLib.IOCondition.IN, ended)
        caller, menu = got
        panel.request("menu " + caller, menu, reply)

    pending = [b""]

    def on_fifo(*_):
        try:
            data = os.read(fifo, 65536)
        except BlockingIOError:
            return True
        lines = (pending[0] + data).split(b"\n")
        pending[0] = lines.pop()
        for line in lines:
            f = line.decode(errors="replace").split()
            if len(f) == 2 and f[0] == "toggle" and f[1] in MODES:
                panel.request(f[1])
            elif f == ["toggle", "launcher"]:
                panel.request("launcher", Launcher(apps.list(), apps.counts()), None)
            elif len(f) == 2 and f[0] == "menu" and f[1].isdigit():
                menu_from(int(f[1]))
            elif f == ["close"]:
                panel.close("chosen")
        return True
    GLib.io_add_watch(GLib.IOChannel.unix_new(fifo), GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, GLib.IOCondition.IN, on_fifo)

    # ended (logout, a restart, Ctrl+C): closed first — the marker goes, the module goes dark
    for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT):
        GLibUnix.signal_add(GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, sig, lambda: Gtk.main_quit() or False)
    # every panel's state read now, so each opens on it at once (read again as it opens)
    for mode in MODES:
        panel.refresh(mode)
    # nothing cold at the first click: every panel and a menu drawn once, off screen, as soon
    # as it is idle (the fonts loaded, Pango's and cairo's caches filled — the first open was
    # the slowest by far), then everything made so far set aside from Python's collector
    # (gc.freeze: no long pause sweeping the startup's objects in the middle of a drag)
    def warm():
        import gc
        if panel.visible:
            return False
        keep = panel.mode
        surf = cairo.ImageSurface(cairo.FORMAT_RGB24, 480, 720)
        for mode in MODES + ("menu", "launcher"):
            if mode == "menu":
                panel.states["menu"] = Menu(["part\t暦\tOctober", "row\t\u25a3\tname\tdetail\t42°", "key\t<\tback"])
            elif mode == "launcher":                   # (the entries read, their icons loaded)
                panel.states["menu"] = Launcher(apps.list(), {})
                mode = "menu"
            panel.mode = mode
            panel.relayout()
            draw(None, cairo.Context(surf))
        panel.mode = keep
        panel.relayout()
        spare()
        gc.collect()
        gc.freeze()
        return False
    GLib.idle_add(warm)
    if want:
        panel.open(want)
    try:
        Gtk.main()
    finally:
        if panel.visible:                              # (its marker gone, its button dark)
            panel.close("ended")
        try:
            if int(open(PIDFILE).read()) == os.getpid():
                os.unlink(PIDFILE)
                os.unlink(FIFO)                       # (no one reads it now)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
